\vspace*{-2ex}

\subsection{\texttt{scikit-learn} interface}

In \texttt{skwdro}, standard models such, as logistic/linear regression, are directly available,
implemented as \texttt{scikit-learn} estimators.
They thus benefit from the same interface and the seamless integration with the rest of \texttt{scikit-learn}'s functionalities such as scoring, pipelining, cross-validation, etc.
As an example, we show below the fitting of \texttt{scikit-learn}'s \texttt{LinearRegression} estimator and its robust counterpart with \texttt{skwdro}.



\lstset{
    escapeinside={(*@}{@*)},
    commentstyle=\color{gray},
    basicstyle=\color{Navy}\ttfamily\footnotesize
}

\begin{lstlisting}[language=Python]
from sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression # sklearn's regressor
from skwdrwo.linear_models import LinearRegression as RobustLinearRegression
X,y     = ... # training data
# (*@\textcolor{BrickRed}{=== ERM ===}@*)
erm_lin = LinearRegression()
erm_lin.fit(X,y)
# (*@\textcolor{ForestGreen}{=== DRO ===}@*)
rob_lin = RobustLinearRegression((*@\textcolor{ForestGreen}{rho}@*)=0.1) # WDRO with radius 0.1
rob_lin.fit(X,y)
\end{lstlisting}





\vspace*{-1ex}

\subsection{\texttt{PyTorch} interface}
\label{subsec:pytorch-interface}

\texttt{skwdro} provides an easy-to-use interface for \texttt{PyTorch} functionalities. In practice, it enables  users to
robustify \texttt{PyTorch} models, with a minimal change in their code. In the example below, the standard training loop (in red) is replaced by its robust counterpart (in green) using \texttt{skwdro}'s wrapper \texttt{robustify}.




\begin{lstlisting}[%
    language=Python,
]
import torch as pt
from skwdro.torch import robustify
model =  # your model as a PyTorch Module
loss_fn = # your loss as a PyTorch Module
(*@\textcolor{ForestGreen}{wdro\_loss = robustify(}@*)
    (*@\textcolor{ForestGreen}{loss\_fn,}@*) (*@\textcolor{ForestGreen}{model,}@*)
    (*@\textcolor{ForestGreen}{rho = 0.1,}@*) # robustness radius
    (*@\textcolor{ForestGreen}{X\_init,}@*) (*@\textcolor{ForestGreen}{y\_init}@*) # provide some sample data for shape initialization
(*@\textcolor{ForestGreen}{)}@*) # replaces the loss of the model by the dual WDRO loss
optimizer = # your choice of optimization method
for epoch in range(num_epochs): # training loop
    for X, y in train_batches:
        optimizer.zero_grad()
        # (*@\textcolor{BrickRed}{=== ERM ===}@*)  Here is what you would usually do to train the model:
        (*@\textcolor{BrickRed}{\#\ loss}@*) = (*@\textcolor{BrickRed}{loss\_fn}@*)(model(X), y).mean()   # standard loss on batch
        (*@\textcolor{BrickRed}{\#\ loss}@*).backward()
        (*@\textcolor{BrickRed}{\#}@*) optimizer.step()  # optimization step
        # (*@\textcolor{ForestGreen}{=== DRO ===}@*) Here is what you would do to train the robust model:
        (*@\textcolor{ForestGreen}{rob\_loss}@*) = (*@\textcolor{ForestGreen}{wdro\_loss}@*)(X, y).mean()  # robust loss
        (*@\textcolor{ForestGreen}{rob\_loss}@*).backward()
        optimizer.step()  # optimization step
\end{lstlisting}



\section{Numerical aspects}\label{sec:issues}


The generic robustification of \texttt{PyTorch} models through the smoothed objective \eqref{eq:EntWDRO} presents some numerical challenges, before even optimizing the model, indeed:

\begin{itemize}
\item Given an implementation of the loss function $\obj_\param(\sample)$, the objective \eqref{eq:EntWDRO} is not yet amenable to stochastic first-order optimization, due to the presence of a (continuous) expectation %
inside the logarithm. We approximate this integral by Monte-Carlo sampling, with a bound on the induced bias.



\item The presence of the exponential in \eqref{eq:EntWDRO} makes the objective sharply peaked, which results in a high variance in the gradient estimate. We mitigate this with importance sampling, shifting the samples following the gradient  $\nabla_{\!\sample}\obj_\param(\sample_i)$.




\item The smoothing depends on some parameters $\sigma$, $\varepsilon$ that have to be tuned.   We provide an automatic calibration for them, based on the problem parameters and the following theoretical guidelines of \citet{azizian2024exact}.

\end{itemize}


Once the model loss is robustified, the user has two options: either use the new loss in their own training/optimization loop, or use the optimization procedure provided by the library (relying on adaptive optimizers \citep{cutkosky2023mechanic}). %






\section{Conclusion}

\texttt{skwdro} is a publicly-available library for \ac{WDRO} machine learning. Through the implementation of the whole pipeline and user interfaces, the goal of the library is to make the training of robust model easy for a wide audience. At the core of the implementation, is a careful numerical work on entropic smoothing of the \ac{WDRO} objective. Numerical illustrations as well as features and implementation details are available in the online documentation at \url{https://skwdro.readthedocs.io}.


