\subsection{Testing Pipeline}
\label{sec:test_pipeline}

During testing, as illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:overview}-(c), the model begins from the known initial bearing state at \(t=0\) and generates predictions for both \(t=0\) and \(t=N\). In the subsequent  iteration, it uses the predicted state at \(t=N\) as input and produces predictions for \(t=N\) and \(t+2N\). Since the state at \(t=N\) is predicted in both iterations, these overlapping predictions are averaged to obtain  a smoothed and more reliable estimate. This process continues -- each iteration producing overlapping predictions that are averaged -- until  the desired final time  is reached. By chaining together these overlapping intervals of length \(N\), the model constructs a stable and consistent  trajectory,  effectively reducing noise and mitigating the accumulation of rollout errors.

