The fact that the running time does not increase proportionately to the number of particles is
explained by the extreme parallelism offered by GPUs. In many cases, the GPU may have enough processing power that reducing the number of particles simply leaves available processing power
idle, which does not lead to speedup. Once the number of particles is large enough that the GPU
must work at full capacity, \ourtool will take longer to run as the number of particles is
increased further. However, the exact speeds and behavior will depend on many factors, most notably
the specific processor, graphics card, drivers, and browser version of the computer running the program.

