\subsection{Price Manipulation Detection}
In this section, we present how \toolname assesses whether the PCM is susceptible to manipulation, leveraging dependency graphs and code slices derived from DeFi contracts.

\subsubsection{Dependency Analysis}
Once the PCM statement is identified, we perform backward data dependency analysis on the price variable to assess whether these dependencies are externally controllable. Given that DeFi contracts are typically open-source, we utilize Slither~\cite{feist2019slither}, a static analysis framework, to perform dependency analysis. We first compile the contract and analyze the price calculation function to build its intra-call graph (ICG). Next, we identify assignment statements related to the price calculation and analyze their dependencies. Specifically, we consider the left-value (lvalue) expression in each assignment depends on the right-value (rvalue) expression and perform a backward traversal to track how the rvalue expression is derived recursively. 
% If the rvalue expression contains a function call, we use the ICG to locate the function definition and analyze the dependencies of its return values recursively. 
Following this, we construct the price calculation dependency graph (PCDG), which provides a structured representation of the data flow influencing price calculation. 
% The PCDG of the motivating example is shown in Figure~\ref{fig:PCDG--PEPG} (a), where the token price is determined by the reserves of two tokens in the liquidity pool, making it susceptible to manipulation. 
Based on the PCDG, we conduct a backward code slicing to extract the relevant slices. 

