Using time window as the granularity of NTWUs and packing multiple time points into a time window has several advantages \cite{PTB}. 
%To address the core issues of complex spatiotemporal dynamics, data disparity between weight and activation, and spatially and temporally unstructured sparsity irregularity, firstly we propose a novel packing method that enables efficient processing. Figure~\ref{fig:tag} illustrates the packing methodology, which consists of three levels of granularity: time stride(TS), time block(TB), and time window(TW). \\
SNNs tend to demonstrate a very high degree of firing sparsity as shown in previous studies \cite{TSSLBP, DL_SNN_2019}. Irregularity of firing patterns, however, renders exploitation of sparsity for weight data reuse and zero-skipping redundant computation challenging.  Packing multiple time points into a time window addresses these two issues. First of all, firing spikes have a strong tendency to cluster as opposed to spread out uniformly in time.  This is reflected in Figure~\ref{fig:dvsg_before_after}, which shows a high degree of sparsity not only at the individual spike level but also in terms of packed time windows, i.e.,  there are only a very few active time windows in which firing spikes cluster. This implies that zero-skipping can be more structurally performed at NTWU level by processing only the NTWUs with a non-zero activity tag.  
Furthermore, as an NTWU is mapped to a PE, the same weight data can be shared to perform synaptic input integration, the most computationally intensive step of the LIF model,  across all time points in the time window. This avoids expensive repeated access to the same weights as would be the case in the conventional time-serial processing where synaptic input integration takes place time step by time step.   


%The clustering effect of spiking neural networks (SNNs) has been demonstrated in previous studies \cite{TSSLBP}. Figure \ref{fig:sparsity_factor} illustrates a high clustering effect especially when spiking activities become denser. Adopting a packing scheme offers two advantages: it allows for the skipping of unstructured zeros and avoids scheduling challenges when we consider the spike sparsity as in shown \cite{Spinalflow}.

\subsection{Agile NTWU Dispatch with Three-Level of Weight Reuse}
The weight data sharing within the time window of a NTWU constitutes the first level of weight data reuse in SpikeX. We explore two higher-level weight data sharing, as illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:Hardware}, by agile dispatch of NTWUs.  
%To address the challenge of costly repeated accesses to large amounts of weight data and low utilization of time-serial processing, we propose a weight-sharing scheme that operates at three levels, as illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:Hardware}(c). The first level of weight reuse involves reusing weights across all time points within a given time window (TW) by a single processing element (PE). The weight register within the PE is repeatedly accessed, avoiding the need for repeated access to more costly cache memories. The second level of weight reuse involves reusing weights across different active TWs for the same post-synaptic neurons in PEs located in different columns and the same row. The third one is, weight is reused for different post-synaptic neurons in some layers such as convolutional layers, so the weight can be reused across different post-synaptic neurons.  The third level of weight reuse involves reusing weights across different post-synaptic neurons, in which weight can be reused in PEs located in different columns. 

