\begin{abstract}
We present an adaptive Finite State Projection (FSP) method for efficiently solving the Chemical Master Equation (CME) with rigorous error control. Our approach integrates time-stepping with dynamic state-space truncation, balancing accuracy and computational cost. Krylov subspace methods approximate the matrix exponential, while quantile-based pruning controls state-space growth by removing low-probability states. Theoretical error bounds ensure that the truncation error remains bounded by the pruneed mass at each step, which is user-controlled, and does not propagate forward in time. An adaptive step size rule further optimizes time-stepping based on local error estimates. Numerical experiments on biochemical systems, including the Lotka–Volterra and Michaelis–Menten models, demonstrate the method’s accuracy and efficiency.
\end{abstract}


% REQUIRED
\begin{keywords}
  chemical master equation, finite state projection, discrete stochastic simulation, stochastic simulation algorithm
\end{keywords}

