\subsubsection{Data Preparation}

We consider the DBLP database, a specialized bibliographic database that provides open bibliographic information on major computational venues with decent coverage and accuracy over Computer Science (CS) literature \cite{rosenfeld2023dblp}. The DBLP database has been widely used in scientometric research and is often deemed as well-representative of the CS community \cite{teddy_ariel_writting_style,dblp_good_1,dblp_good_2,dblp_good_3,dblp_good_4}. We retrieved the complete DBLP dataset on January 20th, 2024. At this point in time, the dataset contained roughly 21.4 million publications from almost 2.6 million scholars which were published in over 16 thousand venues. DBLP indexes several types of publications, most notably journal articles, conference papers, and informal publications. Together, these types cover roughly 90\% of all indexed publications.  For our analysis, we omitted informal publications, such as arXiv preprints, as these are not peer-reviewed works and are only recently sufficiently covered by DBLP\footnote{\url{https://dblp.org/statistics/recordsindblp.html}}  \cite{aviv2024exploring,santos2022discovering}. Aligned with prior work \cite{shir_2,Alexi2024}, we further exclude scholars with less than 5 publications. Overall, our resulting data consists of  0.65 million scholars and 18.3 million publications (\(28.180 \pm 111.607\)) publications per scholar. 

The data from DBLP was then cross-referenced with data from Scopus and JCR. Specifically, scholars' H-indexes and journals' quartile rankings (denoted Q), as of 2022, were retrieved and matched accordingly. In order to reduce author and journal name ambiguity, when multiple records were available, we manually matched the records based on the available information one by one. Overall, we successfully matched 292,158 scholars (91.5\%) and 891 journals, whereas the rest were removed from further consideration to avoid under-quality data. Given the ongoing debate on the ranking and evaluation of other publication types (i.e., conferences, books, etc) \cite{conf_rank_shit_1,conf_rank_shit_2,li2018impact}, we did not assign a counterpart value to the Q ranking for non-journal publications. In cases where a journal is ranked under multiple categories, we used the highest quartile available among the CS categories \cite{shir_1}.  

\subsubsection{Publication and HV Analysis}
Algorithm \ref{algo:hv} outlines the central computation process. Let \(curve\_fit\) be a fitting procedure that receives a publication distribution and an assumed statistical distribution and returns the parameters associated with the optimal fit and its resulting coefficient of determination (\(R^2\)) (in our case, we use the least mean squares method \cite{leastSquares}). In addition, let \(diff\) be a function that computes the numerical differentiation of a given vector (in our case, we use the forward Euler scheme \cite{numerical_diff}). 

\begin{algorithm}[!ht]
\caption{Identifying Home Venues}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\State \textbf{Input:} $H_t^s$
\State \textbf{Output:} Number of HVs ($h$)

\State \(dist_{sp}, R^2_{sp} \leftarrow curve\_fit(H_t^s, \text{\say{Single-Peak}})\)
\State \(dist_p, R^2_p \leftarrow curve\_fit(H_t^s, \text{\say{Pareto}})\)
\State \(dist_l, R^2_l \leftarrow curve\_fit(H_t^s, \text{\say{Linear}})\)
\State \(dist_u, R^2_u \leftarrow curve\_fit(H_t^s, \text{\say{Uniform}})\)
\State \(Best = argmax\{R^2_{sp},R^2_p,R^2_l,R^2_u\}\)
\If{\(Best = 'Single-Peak'\)}
    \State \textbf{Return} \text{the single venue of $H_t^s$}
\EndIf
\If{\(Best = 'Pareto'\)}
    \State $h \leftarrow argmin(diff(dist_p, 2))$
\Else
    \State $h \leftarrow 0$
\EndIf
\State \textbf{Return} \text{first $h$ venues in $H_t^s$} 
\end{algorithmic}
\label{algo:hv}
\end{algorithm}

% Importantly, in order to show that the HV's definition is well-defined, one should show that if a sample, \(\{x_i\}_{i=1}^{n}\) which indicates the number of publications in each venue is distributed according to a non-Pareto distribution, then the number of HVs would be equal to zero. Hence, let us assume that a given sample, \(\{x_i\}_{i=1}^{n}\), is distributed uniformly such that each venue has exactly \(c \in \mathbf{N}\) publications. In order to fit a Pareto distribution to this data, one needs to find two parameters - \(x_m\) and \(\alpha\). By definition, \(x_m := \min_{i \in N} x_i\) and \(\alpha = \frac{n}{\sum_{i=1}^{n} ln(x_i/x_m)}\). For our case, \(x_m = c\) and \(\alpha\) are undefined as the denominator equals zero. Hence, in such a situation, one of the HV conditions is not met and therefore there are no HVs, as needed. Next, let us consider the case where the samples, \(\{x_i\}_{i=1}^{n}\), are distributed in a linear fashion; such that \(\exists a, b \forall i \in [1, \dots, n]: x_i = ai + b\), assuming the samples are ordered from the smallest to the largest. In this case, if \(a, b > 0\), the Pareto distribution can approximate the given sample. As such, one would obtain those venuses as HVs, according to the definition, if the CFD up to \(arg min(CFD'')\) multiplied by the number of venues (\(n\)) is larger than 1. As such, we check when such condition is not met, obtaining that for \(n \leq \frac{m}{4b}(b + \sqrt{b^2 - 8\frac{b}{m}})\) where \(m := \sum_{i=1}^n ln(\frac{a(i-1)}{b} + 1)\). Thus, the HVs definition is well-defined. 

First, we apply Algorithm \ref{algo:hv} to each scholar in the dataset considering his/her entire publication distribution. Then, for each scholar with a non-empty set of HVs, we explore the dynamics and characteristics of his/her HVs. We consider the partial publication distributions starting from the first five publications onwards and re-apply Algorithm \ref{algo:hv} to identify the periods for which HVs exist. A given publication is deemed a HVs \say{emergence} point if the scholar's HVs set becomes non-empty as the result of that publication and remains non-empty thereafter. Second, we explore the number, types, and rankings of HVs with and without considering their scholars' H-indexes and number of publications using standard statistical testing. 
