\subsection{Statistical Analysis of Performance Metrics}

To ascertain the statistical significance of the differences observed in performance metrics, we conducted hypothesis testing for both the number of vehicles passed and average wait time.

\subsubsection{Vehicles Passed/ Throughput}

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Normality Tests:} The Shapiro-Wilk test was employed to evaluate the normality of the data distributions, yielding p-values of 0.225 for the fixed-time controller and 0.053 for the MARL controller. These results suggest that the data for both controllers adheres to an approximately normal distribution.

    \item \textbf{Variance Homogeneity:} Levene's test was performed to assess the homogeneity of variances, resulting in a p-value of 0.641. This indicates that the variances between the two groups can be considered equal.

    \item \textbf{Independent Samples t-Test:} The independent samples t-test revealed a t-statistic of -14.9612, accompanied by a p-value of $1.64 \times 10^{-17}$. This indicates a statistically significant difference in the number of vehicles passed between the two controllers.

    \item \textbf{Effect Size:} The effect size was calculated using Cohen's d, which yielded a value of 4.7311. This signifies a very large effect size, suggesting a meaningful impact of the MARL controller.

    \item \textbf{Mann-Whitney U Test:} To further corroborate the findings, the Mann-Whitney U Test was conducted, yielding a U-statistic of 0.0000 with a p-value of $5.22 \times 10^{-8}$. This result aligns with the conclusions drawn from the t-test.

    \item \textbf{Bootstrap 95\% Confidence Interval:} Finally, a bootstrap analysis was performed, generating a 95\% confidence interval for the mean difference, which ranged from [5.90, 7.60]. This interval reinforces the statistical significance found in the comparative assessments.
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Wait Time}

In this section, we analyze the wait times associated with the fixed-time controller and the MARL controller. 

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Normality Tests:} To assess the normality of the wait time distributions, we conducted the Shapiro-Wilk test. The results yielded p-values of 0.537 for the fixed-time controller and 0.679 for the MARL controller, indicating that both datasets follow a normal distribution.

    \item \textbf{Variance Homogeneity:} We performed Levene's test to evaluate the equality of variances between the two groups. The test produced a p-value of $3.50 \times 10^{-4}$, which suggests that the variances are significantly unequal.

    \item \textbf{Welch's t-Test:} Given the evidence of unequal variances, we utilized Welch's t-test to compare the mean wait times. The analysis resulted in a t-statistic of 209.1100 and a p-value of $8.59 \times 10^{-39}$, indicating a highly significant difference between the wait times of the two controllers.

    \item \textbf{Effect Size:} To quantify the magnitude of this difference, we calculated Cohen's d, which was found to be -66.1264. This value suggests an extremely large effect size, emphasizing the practical significance of the findings.

    \item \textbf{Mann-Whitney U Test:} Further supporting our results, the Mann-Whitney U test yielded a U-statistic of 400.0000 and a p-value of $3.40 \times 10^{-8}$. This non-parametric test corroborates the findings of the Welch's t-test, reinforcing the evidence for a significant difference.

    \item \textbf{Bootstrap 95\% Confidence Interval:} Finally, we constructed a bootstrap 95\% confidence interval for the mean difference in wait times, which yielded the interval [-4154.47, -4078.52]. This interval further confirms the substantial reduction in wait time associated with the MARL controller.
\end{itemize}

