%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%% Abstract %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\abstract{We present the first step in the development of an Adaptive Mesh Refinement (AMR) solver for coastal engineering applications, based on a high-order Discontinuous Galerkin (DG) method as implemented in the \texttt{deal.II} library. This environment provides efficient and native parallelization techniques and automatically handles non-conforming meshes to implement both static and dynamic AMR approaches. The proposed method is automatically well-balanced, allows the use of realistic bathymetry data without any regularity assumption, and includes a consistent conservative discretization for transported chemical species. Numerical experiments on idealized benchmarks validate the proposed approach, while results obtained on realistic bathymetries and complex domains show its potential for accurate and efficient adaptive simulations of coastal flows.}

\pagebreak

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%% Introduction %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

The shallow water equations are a simplified model of hydrostatic flows at small depth-to-width aspect ratio, which is widely used in river hydraulics and coastal engineering. A rigorous derivation of this model from the incompressible Navier-Stokes equations can be found, e.g., in \cite{bresch:2007, decoene:2009}. A great number of different approximation techniques for their numerical solution has been proposed in the past, often tailored to address adequately the specific difficulties posed by different flow regimes. For low Froude number regimes, which will be the focus of this work, efficient semi-implicit finite difference methods were proposed, e.g., in the seminal papers \cite{casulli:1990, casulli:1992}. These methods are extremely popular in the coastal engineering community and they have been extended to high-order finite elements, e.g., in \cite{busto:2022, dumbser:2013, tumolo:2013}. The purpose of this work is to present the first step in the development of an adaptive solver for the shallow water equations for coastal engineering applications, which also inherits the efficiency and robustness properties of the methods introduced in \cite{casulli:1990, casulli:1992}.

The proposed spatial discretization is based on a high order Discontinuous Galerkin (DG) approach. The application of the DG method for the numerical solution of the shallow water equations has been performed by many authors since the early 2000s, on Cartesian \cite{tassi:2007, xing:2010} and on unstructured meshes, see \cite{giraldo:2002, kubatko:2006} among many others. We have used the DG implementation of the \texttt{deal.II} library \cite{arndt:2023, bangerth:2007}. This modelling framework has proven to be extremely useful for the implementation of numerical models for environmental flows, see, e.g., \cite{orlando:2022, orlando:2023a, orlando:2024}. The \texttt{deal.II} library provides efficient and native parallelization techniques and automatically handles non-conforming meshes to implement both static and dynamic Adaptive Mesh Refinement (AMR) approaches with excellent scalability results, as shown in the previously cited papers. A numerical method for the shallow water equations based on \texttt{deal.II} was also recently proposed in \cite{guermond:2025}. However, the approach described in this work differs from that of \cite{guermond:2025} in terms of model equations, numerical discretization, and implementation, as we will briefly discuss in the following.

Our longer term goal is to implement efficient Implicit-Explicit Runge-Kutta (IMEX-RK) time discretization schemes with an implicit treatment of the pressure gradient terms, as already done in \cite{orlando:2022, orlando:2023a} for atmospheric models, thus guaranteeing time step restrictions based on velocity rather than celerity values. However, in this work we will restrict our attention to IMEX-RK time discretization methods that treat the pressure gradient terms explicitly, while applying the implicit scheme to the frictional terms, that can become very stiff when realistic friction models are employed on strongly varying bathymetries.

In this high-order framework, we focus on the treatment of the bathymetry, nowadays available with higher resolution than the mesh, in coastal areas. This poses a series of challenges for higher order methods that work on coarse meshes: the mesh may not be aligned to large bathymetric gradients and the finite element method should be able to handle such gradients within an element or along an edge. We propose a robust and accurate approach that uses the realistic bathymetry data without any regularity assumption. Similarly to the formulation considered in \cite{casulli:2009}, we choose as prognostic variable the free-surface elevation $\zeta$ that, in subcritical regimes, is a smooth variable and thus suited for a finite element representation. The bathymetry $z_{b}$ at the quadrature node is directed evaluated from the reference data without any modification. With respect to the conservation of mass, if the water depth is strictly positive, evolving the water depth or the free-surface elevation is equivalent. This is no longer true in presence of wetting and drying regions, because of the nonlinear relationship $h = \max\rpth{\zeta + z_{b},0}$. The choice of evolving the water depth assures mass-conservation straightforwardly and, for this reason, it is the preferred choice for flooding applications, for DG see \cite{bunya:2009, filippini:2024, vater:2019}. However, a mass-conserving formulation that still keeps the free-surface elevation as prognostic variable was proposed in \cite{casulli:2009} and will be the basis for our treatment of wetting and drying in future work. For the purposes of this paper, we assume that $h > 0$ throughout the considered time interval and spatial domain. As in \cite{casulli:2009} we consider the hydrostatic pressure gradient in a non-conservative form. This choice is motivated by the fact that, as it will be shown, the hydrostatic well-balancing, known in the literature as the {\cal C}-property \cite{vazquez:1999}, follows automatically also in a high-order finite element context with this formulation. This is fully justified in the low-Froude regimes that are the target applications of the present work. Two non-conservative formulations of the pressure gradient are considered, introduced in \cite{orlando:2023b, tumolo:2015,}. Their relationship to the standard conservative formulation is discussed in detail, showing that they are equivalent for smooth bathymetries.

These choices yield a finite element discretization different with respect to well-balanced \cite{ricchiuto:2009, vazquez:1999, xing:2010} or pre-balanced formulations \cite{kurganov:2002, rogers:2001}, for which the same polynomial representation of the water depth and bathymetry is employed. Hence, in the case of higher order polynomials and irregular bathymetry, smoothing or TVD limiting of the bathymetry is then needed to avoid or cure the well-known Gibbs phenomenon.

Following the analysis in \cite{gross:2002, lin:1996}, we take special care to guarantee that the conservative discretizations of the continuity and tracer concentration equations are consistent. Failure of this consistency with continuity (CWC) property was shown in \cite{gross:2002} to cause macroscopic violations of monotonicity even for completely monotonic methods. Indeed, we show that, for a non-polynomial bathymetry, a naif conservative update of the depth-integrated concentrations will fail to guarantee such a consistency. On the other hand, a consistent discretization that guarantees this property also for arbitrary bathymetries is proposed.

We test for the first time static and dynamic AMR based on the non-conforming meshes handled by \texttt{deal.II} for coastal engineering purposes. Well known applications of the AMR approach to the shallow water equations have been presented, among many others, in \cite{leveque:2011, popinet:2011} and, with DG, in \cite{gerhard:2015, kesserwani:2023}. As discussed, e.g., in \cite{arpaia:2018, donat:2014, liang:2015, popinet:2011}, when performing dynamic adaptation with non-conforming meshes, guaranteeing simultaneously well-balancing and mass conservation is not straightforward. The discretization proposed here guarantees automatically well-balancing also in the case of dynamical AMR, while mass conservation is violated in that case. This is consistent with our focus on demonstrating the potential efficiency gain of dynamic AMR approaches for short term simulations, for which mass conservation is less relevant.

Numerical results obtained on idealized benchmarks validate the robustness and accuracy of the proposed method in presence of irregular bathymetry also with under-resolved features at
the grid scale. Simulations of coastal flows using realistic and complex domain geometries and bathymetries demonstrate the effectiveness of the resulting discretization as compared to state of the art coastal flow models and its good parallel performance.

The outline of the paper is the following. In Section \ref{sec:modeleq}, the model equations are discussed. Their numerical approximation is introduced in Section \ref{sec:numerical} and the validation of the \texttt{deal.II} implementation of these methods, based on a number of relevant benchmarks of increasing complexity, is presented in Section \ref{sec:tests}. Moreover, we consider a realistic bathymetry and a complex geometry in Section \ref{sec:realistic}, where we report results of semi-realistic simulations of the tidal circulations in the Venice Lagoon. A parallel efficiency analysis of the implementation, including dynamic AMR, is presented in Section \ref{sec:parallel}. Some conclusions and perspectives for the future developments mentioned before are reported in Section \ref{sec:conclu}, while a more detailed description of the time discretization schemes is reported in Appendix \ref{app:IMEX_coeffs}.

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%% Model equations %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{Model equations}
\label{sec:modeleq}

We consider the shallow water equations in the domain $\mathbf{x} \in \Omega$, $t \in (0,T],$ which can be formulated as
%
\begin{eqnarray}\label{eq:shw} 
    &&\pad{\zeta}{t} + \dive\mathbf{q} = 0 \nonumber \\
    &&\pad{\mathbf{q}}{t} + \dive\rpth{\mathbf{q} \otimes \frac{\mathbf{q}}{h}} = -gh\grad\zeta - \gamma\rpth{q, h}\mathbf{q} \\
    &&\pad{hc_{k}}{t} + \dive\rpth{\mathbf{q}c_{k}} = h \phi_{k}\rpth{c_{1},\dots,c_{M}}, \quad k=1,\dots,M, \nonumber
\end{eqnarray}
%
endowed with suitable initial and boundary conditions. Here $T$ is the final time, $\zeta$ denotes the free-surface elevation, whereas $z_{b}$ denotes the bathymetry depth, both computed with respect to a fixed reference height, so that $h = \zeta + z_{b}$ is the total fluid depth. We denote by $\mathbf{q} = (hu, hv)^{\top}$ the mass flux or discharge per unit area, while $\vel = (u, v)^{\top} = \mathbf{q}/h$ denotes the vector of vertically averaged velocities and $\gamma$ denotes a non-negative friction coefficient, which will depend on the absolute value of the discharge $q = |\mathbf{q}|$ and on the fluid depth $h$. More specifically, we will consider the friction model
%
\begin{equation}\label{eq:manning}
    \gamma\rpth{q,h} = \frac{g n^2 q}{h^{\frac{7}{3}}},
\end{equation}
%
where $n$ denotes the so-called Manning friction coefficient.
The quantities $c_{k}, k=1,\dots,M$ represent the concentrations of $M$ chemical or biological species with reaction rates $\phi_{k}$, whose motion is determined by that of the fluid. Finally, $g = \SI{9.81}{\meter\per\second\squared}$ is the gravitational acceleration. Notice that no assumption is made on the regularity of the bathymetry $z_{b}$ that can be discontinuous.

Similarly to the formulation considered in \cite{casulli:2009}, system \eqref{eq:shw} has two main differences with respect to well-balanced \cite{guermond:2025, ricchiuto:2009, vazquez:1999} or pre-balanced formulations \cite{kurganov:2002, rogers:2001}. A first main difference is the use of the free-surface elevation $\zeta $ as prognostic variable. As already remarked, in subcritical regimes, $\zeta$ is a smooth variable and thus suited for a finite element representation. A second key difference with respect to other standard formulations is that system \eqref{eq:shw} considers the hydrostatic pressure gradient in a non-conservative form. As it will be shown, hydrostatic well balancing follows automatically also in a high-order finite element context with this formulation. 

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%% Numerical discretization %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{Numerical discretization}
\label{sec:numerical}

In this Section, we outline the numerical methods employed for the spatial and temporal discretization of system \eqref{eq:shw}
and we analyze in detail the treatment of the non conservative free-surface gradients and the CWC property of the resulting scheme.

\subsection{Time discretization}
\label{ssec:timedisc}

For the time discretization, an IMplicit-EXplicit (IMEX) Runge-Kutta (RK) method \cite{kennedy:2003} will be used. These methods are useful for time dependent problems that can be formulated as $\mathbf{y}^{'} = \mathbf{f}_{S}(\mathbf{y},t) + \mathbf{f}_{NS}(\mathbf{y},t)$, where the $S$ and $NS$ subscripts denote the stiff and non-stiff components of the system, respectively, to which the implicit and explicit companion methods are applied. IMEX-RK methods are represented compactly by the two Butcher tableaux \cite{butcher:2008}:
%
\begin{center}
    \begin{tabular}{c|c}
	$\mathbf{c}$ & $\mathbf{A}$ \\
	\hline
        \rule{0pt}{2.5ex}
	& $\mathbf{b}^{\top}$
    \end{tabular}
    %
    \qquad
    %
    \begin{tabular}{c|c}
	$\tilde{\mathbf{c}}$ & $\tilde{\mathbf{A}}$  \\
	\hline
        \rule{0pt}{2.5ex}
	& $\tilde{\mathbf{b}}^{\top}$
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
%
with $\mathbf{A} = \cpth{a_{lm}}, \mathbf{b} = \cpth{b_{l}}, \mathbf{c} = \cpth{c_{l}}, \tilde{\mathbf{A}} = \cpth{\tilde{a}_{lm}}, \tilde{\mathbf{b}} = \cpth{\tilde{b}_{l}}$, and $\tilde{\mathbf{c}} = \cpth{\tilde{c}_{l}}$. Coefficients $a_{lm}, b_{l}$, and $c_{l}$ denote the coefficients of the explicit companion method, while $\tilde{a}_{lm}, \tilde{b}_{l}$, and $\tilde{c}_l$ denote the coefficients of the implicit companion method. The coefficients are determined so that the method is consistent of a given order. In particular, in addition to the order conditions specific to each sub-method, the coefficients should respect specific coupling conditions, as discussed in detail in \cite{pareschi:2005}, to which we refer for a complete presentation (see also the discussion in Appendix \ref{app:IMEX_coeffs}). We consider Diagonal Implicitly Runge-Kutta (DIRK) methods for the implicit companion scheme, so that $\tilde{a}_{lm} = 0$ for $l < m$. If $\mathbf{v}^{n} \approx \mathbf{y}(t^{n})$, the generic $l-$stage IMEX-RK method can be defined therefore as
%
\begin{equation}
    \begin{array}{rcl}
        \mathbf{v}^{(n,l)} = \mathbf{v}^{n} &+& 
        \Delta t \sum\limits_{m=1}^{l-1} \spth{a_{lm}\mathbf{f}_{NS}\rpth{\mathbf{v}^{(n,m)},t + c_{m}\Delta t} + \tilde{a}_{lm}\mathbf{f}_{S}\rpth{\mathbf{v}^{(n,m)},t + \tilde{c}_{m}\Delta t}} \\
        &+& \Delta t \, \tilde{a}_{ll} \, \mathbf{f}_{S}\rpth{\mathbf{v}^{(n,l)}, t + \tilde{c}_l\Delta t},
    \end{array}
    \nonumber 
\end{equation}
%
where $l=1, \dots, s$. After the computation of the intermediate stages, $\mathbf{v}^{n+1}$ is computed as
%
$$\mathbf{v}^{n+1} = \mathbf{v}^{n} + \Delta t \sum_{l=1}^{s}\spth{b_{l} \mathbf{f}_{NS}\rpth{\mathbf{v}^{(n,l)}, t + c_{l}\Delta t} + \tilde{b}_{l}\mathbf{f}_{S}\rpth{\mathbf{v}^{(n,l)},t + \tilde{c}_{l}\Delta t}}.$$
%
The generic semi-discrete IMEX-RK stage for system \eqref{eq:shw} with implicit treatment of the friction term then reads as follows:
%
\begin{eqnarray}\label{eq:imex_shw}
    \zeta^{(n,l)} &=& \zeta^{n} - \Delta t\sum_{m=1}^{l-1} a_{lm}\dive\mathbf{q}^{(n,m)} \nonumber \\
    %
    h^{(n,l)} &=& \zeta^{(n,l)} + z_{b} \nonumber \\
    %
    \mathbf{q}^{(n,l)} &=& \mathbf{q}^{n} - \Delta t\sum_{m=1}^{l-1} a_{lm}\dive\rpth{\mathbf{q}^{(n,m)} \otimes \frac{\mathbf{q}^{(n,m)}}{h^{(n,m)}}} \nonumber \\
    &-& \Delta t\sum_{m=1}^{l-1}a_{lm}g h^{(n,m)}\grad\zeta^{(n,m)} \\
    &-& \Delta t\sum_{m=1}^{l-1}\tilde{a}_{lm}\gamma\rpth{q^{(n,m)},h^{(n,m)}}\mathbf{q}^{(n,m)} - \Delta t\tilde{a}_{ll}\gamma\rpth{q^{(n,l-1)}, h^{(n,l-1)}}\mathbf{q}^{(n,l)} \nonumber \\
    %
    h^{(n,l)} c_{k}^{(n,l)} &=& \nonumber h^{n}c_{k}^{n} - \Delta t\sum_{m=1}^{l-1} a_{lm} \dive\rpth{\mathbf{q}^{(n,m)}c_{k}^{(n,m)}} \nonumber \\
    &+& \Delta t\sum_{m=1}^{l-1} a_{lm} h^{(n,m)} \phi_{k}\rpth{c_{1}^{(n,m)},\dots,c_{M}^{(n,m)}}, \qquad k = 1 \dots M. \nonumber
\end{eqnarray}
%
Notice that the source terms representing biogeochemical reactions have been discretized in time with the explicit part of the IMEX method. In most commonly used biogeochemical models, these terms are non-stiff and often simply discretized by the forward Euler method in practical applications. However, inclusion of stiff chemical reactions discretized by the implicit part of the IMEX scheme would be possible along the same lines, resulting in a set of decoupled $M \times M$ nonlinear systems for each discrete degree of freedom. The full discretization of these terms will not be considered further in this paper, but it can be included without changes in the general structure of the proposed method and will be taken into account in more realistic simulations to be carried out in future work.

The nonlinear dependence of the friction term on the discharge and fluid depth values is linearized in time, so as to avoid the solution of a nonlinear system at each time step. Since the stability restrictions based on celerity do not allow the use of very large time steps for this approach and considering also the uncertainties associated with the empirical friction formulae usually employed to define $\gamma$, this simplification does not imply a significant loss of accuracy. Furthermore, similar simplifications were performed in \cite{tumolo:2015} in the context of large time step, high-order DG methods without degrading the accuracy of the results. All the details of the Butcher tableaux used for the time discretization are reported in Appendix \ref{app:IMEX_coeffs}.

We stress the fact that we solve for the concentration and not for the depth-integrated concentration, as it would be natural for a conservative formulation. As explained later in this Section, this allows us to obtain a discretization that is consistent with that of the continuity equation. This choice also simplifies the implementation of the initial and boundary conditions, as well as the output of the concentration variables.

\subsection{Spatial discretization}
\label{ssec:spacedisc}

For the spatial discretization, we employ a standard nodal DG method \cite{giraldo:2020}, which is implemented in the framework of the \texttt{deal.II} library \cite{arndt:2023, bangerth:2007}. We consider a decomposition of the domain $\Omega$ into a family of quadrilaterals $\mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{H}}$ and denote each element by $K$ with mesh size $\mathcal{H}_{K}$. The skeleton $\mathcal{E}$ denotes the set of all element faces and $\mathcal{E} = \mathcal{E}^{I} \cup \mathcal{E}^{B}$, where $\mathcal{E}^{I}$ is the subset of interior faces and $\mathcal{E}^{B}$ is the subset of boundary faces. Then, suitable jump and average operators have to be defined for discontinuous finite element discretizations. A face $\Gamma \in \mathcal{E}^{I}$ shares two elements that we denote by $K^{+}$ with outward unit normal $\mathbf{n}^{+}$ and $K^{-}$ with outward unit normal $\mathbf{n}^{-}$, whereas for a face $\Gamma \in \mathcal{E}^{B}$ we denote by $\mathbf{n}$ the outward unit normal. For a scalar function $\varphi$ the jump is defined as
%
$$\jump{\varphi} = \varphi^{+}\mathbf{n}^{+} + \varphi^{-}\mathbf{n}^{-} \quad \text{if }\Gamma \in \mathcal{E}^{I} \qquad \jump{\varphi} = \varphi\mathbf{n} \quad \text{if }\Gamma \in \mathcal{E}^{B}.$$
%
The average is defined as
%
$$\ave{\varphi} = \frac{1}{2}\rpth{\varphi^{+} + \varphi^{-}} \quad \text{if }\Gamma \in \mathcal{E}^{I} \qquad \ave{\varphi} = \varphi \quad \text{if }\Gamma \in \mathcal{E}^{B}.$$
%
Similar definitions apply for a vector function $\boldsymbol{\varphi}$:
%
\begin{align*}
    &\jump{\boldsymbol{\varphi}} = \boldsymbol{\varphi}^{+}\cdot\mathbf{n}^{+} + \boldsymbol{\varphi}^{-}\cdot\mathbf{n}^{-} \quad \text{if }\Gamma \in \mathcal{E}^{I} \qquad 
    \jump{\boldsymbol{\varphi}} = \boldsymbol{\varphi}\cdot\mathbf{n} \quad \text{if }\Gamma \in \mathcal{E}^{B} \\
    %
    &\ave{\boldsymbol{\varphi}} = \frac{1}{2}\rpth{\boldsymbol{\varphi}^{+} + \boldsymbol{\varphi}^{-}} \quad \text{if }\Gamma \in \mathcal{E}^{I} \qquad \ave{\boldsymbol{\varphi}} = \boldsymbol{\varphi} \quad \text{if }\Gamma \in \mathcal{E}^{B}.
\end{align*}
%
For vector functions, it is also useful to define a tensor jump as:
%
$$\tjump{\boldsymbol{\varphi}} = \boldsymbol{\varphi}^{+}\otimes\mathbf{n}^{+} + \boldsymbol{\varphi}^{-}\otimes\mathbf{n}^{-} \quad \text{if }\Gamma \in \mathcal{E}^{I} 
\qquad \tjump{\boldsymbol{\varphi}} = \boldsymbol{\varphi}\otimes\mathbf{n} \quad \text{if }\Gamma \in \mathcal{E}^{B}.$$
%
We also introduce the following finite element spaces
%
$$Q_{r} = \cpth{v \in L^2(\Omega) : v\rvert_K \in \mathbb{Q}_{r} \quad \forall K \in \mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{H}}} \qquad \mathbf{V}_{r} = \spth{Q_{r}}^{d},$$
%
where $\mathbb{Q}_{r}$ is the space of polynomials of degree $r$ in each coordinate direction. We then denote by $\boldsymbol{\varphi}_{i}(\mathbf{x})$ the basis functions for the space $\mathbf{V}_{r}$ and by $\psi_i(\mathbf{x})$ the basis functions for the space $Q_{r}$, the finite element spaces chosen for the discretization of the discharge and of the free-surface height (as well as the concentration of the chemical species), respectively, so that
%
$$\mathbf{q} \approx \sum_{j = 1}^{d\left|\mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{H}}\right|(r+1)^{d}}q_j(t)\boldsymbol{\varphi}_j(\mathbf{x}) \qquad \zeta \approx \sum_{j = 1}^{\left|\mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{H}}\right|(r+1)^{d}}\zeta_{j}(t)\psi_{j}(\mathbf{x}).$$
%
The shape functions correspond to the products of Lagrange interpolation polynomials for the support points of $\rpth{r + 1}$-order Gauss-Lobatto quadrature rule in each coordinate direction. Notice that, while equal polynomial degrees have been used for the discretization of the free-surface and discharge variables, the implementation framework would easily allow to use a higher polynomial degree for the discharge if required by specific applications.

Furthermore, it is important to remark that the total fluid depth $h$ is not expanded in terms of the basis functions, but rather computed only at the quadrature nodes using the second equation in \eqref{eq:imex_shw}. This allows us to use non-polynomial representations of the bathymetry.
We will further discuss this point in Sections \ref{ssec:lake_at_rest_pert} and \ref{ssec:channel_friction}.

Given these definitions, the algebraic system for the continuity equation \eqref{eq:imex_shw} at each stage reads as follows:
%
\begin{equation}\label{eq:algebraic_elevation}
    \mathbf{M}\boldsymbol{\zeta}^{(n,l)} = \mathbf{M}\boldsymbol{\zeta}^{n} + \Delta t \sum_{m=1}^{l-1} a_{lm}\mathbf{F}^{(n,m)}.
\end{equation}
%
Here, $\mathbf{M}$ denotes the mass matrix, $\boldsymbol{\zeta}^{(n,l)}$ denotes the vector of the degrees of freedom associated to the free-surface height, and $\mathbf{F}^{(n,l)}$ represents the DG approximation of the right-hand side of the continuity equation at previous stages according to \eqref{eq:imex_shw}. More specifically, we have set
%
\begin{subequations}
\begin{eqnarray}
    M_{ij} &=& \sum_{K \in \mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{H}}} \int_{K} \psi_{j}\psi_{i} \mathrm{d}\Omega \label{eq:mass_matrix} \\
    %
    F_{i}^{(n,m)} &=& \sum_{K \in \mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{H}}} \int_{K} \mathbf{q}^{(n,m)} \cdot \grad\psi_{i} \mathrm{d}\Omega \nonumber \\
    &-& \sum_{\Gamma \in \mathcal{E}} \int_{\Gamma} \rpth{\ave{\mathbf{q}^{(n,m)}} - \frac{\lambda^{(n,m)}}{2}\jump{\zeta^{(n,m)}}} \cdot \jump{\psi_{i}} \mathrm{d}\Sigma. \label{eq:mass_rhs}
\end{eqnarray}
\end{subequations}
%
Notice that, for discontinuous finite elements, the mass matrix is block diagonal, since the support of the test functions associated to a given element $K$ is restricted to that element. The integral at the interface between two elements is computed with a Rusanov flux \cite{rusanov:1962}, so that:
%
\begin{eqnarray}
    \lambda^{(n,m)} = \max\rpth{\left|\vel^{+,(n,m)} \cdot \mathbf{n}^{+}\right| + \sqrt{gh^{+,(n,m)}}, \left|\vel^{-,(n,m)} \cdot \mathbf{n}^{-}\right| + \sqrt{gh^{-,(n,m)}}}
\end{eqnarray}
%
Analogously, the algebraic system for the discharge equation at each stage reads as follows:
%
\begin{equation}\label{eq:algebraic_discharge}
    \mathbf{A}^{(n,l)}\mathbf{Q}^{(n,l)} = \tilde{\mathbf{Q}}^{n} + \Delta t \sum_{m=1}^{l-1}a_{lm}\mathbf{G}^{(n,m)} + \Delta t\sum_{m=1}^{l-1}\tilde{a}_{lm}\mathbf{L}^{(n,m)},
\end{equation}
%
$\mathbf{Q}^{(n,l)}$ denotes the vector of the degrees of freedom associated to the discharge field, $\mathbf{A}^{(n,m)}$ denotes a modified mass-matrix, $\mathbf{G}^{(n,l)}$ and $\mathbf{L}^{(n,l)}$ represents the DG approximation of the non-stiff and stiff components of right-hand side of the discharge equation according to \eqref{eq:imex_shw} and evaluated at the previous stages. Here we have therefore set
%
\begin{subequations}
\begin{eqnarray}
    A_{ij}^{(n,m)} &=& \sum_{K \in \mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{H}}} \int_{K} \spth{1 + \tilde{a}_{ll}\Delta t\gamma\rpth{q^{(n,l-1)},h^{(n,l-1)}}}\boldsymbol{\varphi}_{j} \cdot \boldsymbol{\varphi}_{i} \mathrm{d}\Omega \label{eq:mass_matrix_discharge} \\
    %
    \tilde{Q}_{i}^{n} &=& \sum_{K \in \mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{H}}} \int_{K} \mathbf{q}^{n} \cdot \boldsymbol{\varphi}_{i} \mathrm{d}\Omega \\
    %
    G_{i}^{(n,m)} &=& \sum_{K \in \mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{H}}} \int_{K} \mathbf{q}^{(n,m)} \otimes \frac{\mathbf{q}^{(n,m)}}{h^{(n,m)}} : \grad\boldsymbol{\varphi}_{i} \mathrm{d}\Omega \nonumber \\
    &-& \sum_{\Gamma \in \mathcal{E}} \int_{\Gamma} \rpth{\ave{\mathbf{q}^{(n,m)} \otimes \frac{\mathbf{q}^{(n,m)}}{h^{(n,m)}}} + \frac{\lambda^{(n,m)}}{2}\tjump{\mathbf{q}^{(n,m)}}} : \tjump{\boldsymbol{\varphi}_{i}} \mathrm{d}\Sigma \nonumber \\
    &-& \sum_{K \in \mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{H}}} \int_{K}g h^{(n,m)}\grad\zeta^{(n,m)} \cdot \boldsymbol{\varphi}_{i} \mathrm{d}\Omega \\
    &-& \sum_{\Gamma \in \mathcal{E}} \int_{\Gamma} \rpth{g\ave{h^{(n,m)}}\ave{\zeta^{(n,m)}}\jump{\boldsymbol{\varphi}_{i}} - g\ave{h^{(n,m)}}\jump{\zeta^{(n,m)}\boldsymbol{\varphi}_{i}}} \mathrm{d}\Sigma 
    \nonumber
    \\
    %
    L_{i}^{(n,m)} &=& - \sum_{K \in \mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{H}}} \int_{K} \gamma\rpth{q^{(n,m)}, h^{(n,m)}}\mathbf{q}^{(n,m)} \cdot \boldsymbol{\varphi}_{i} \mathrm{d}\Omega.
\end{eqnarray}
\end{subequations}
%
The algebraic formulation for the equations of the concentrations at each stage can be written in a similar manner
%
\begin{equation}\label{eq:algebraic_concentration}
    \mathbf{H}^{(n,l)}\mathbf{C}^{(n,l)} = \tilde{\mathbf{C}}^{n} + \Delta t \sum_{m=1}^{l-1}a_{lm}\mathbf{R}^{(n,m)},
\end{equation}
%
where now the reaction source terms have been omitted. Here, $\mathbf{C}^{(n,l)}$ denotes the vector of the degrees of freedom associated to the  concentration $c$ of the generic species. $\mathbf{H}^{(n,m)}$ denotes a modified mass matrix where we have condensed the water depth and $\mathbf{R}^{(n,l)}$ represents the DG approximation of the right-hand side of the concentration equation at previous stages according to \eqref{eq:imex_shw}. More precisely,
%
\begin{subequations}
\begin{eqnarray}
    H^{(n,l)}_{ij} &=& \sum_{K \in \mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{H}}} \int_{K} h^{(n,l)}\psi_{j}\psi_{i} \mathrm{d}\Omega \label{eq:mass_matrix_concentration} \\
    %
    \tilde{C}_{i}^{n} &=& \sum_{K \in \mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{H}}} \int_{K} h^{n}c^{n}\psi_{i} \mathrm{d}\Omega \\
    %
    R_{i}^{(n,m)} &=& \sum_{K \in \mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{H}}} \int_{K} \mathbf{q}^{(n,m)}c^{(n,m)} \cdot \grad\psi_{i} \mathrm{d}\Omega \nonumber \\
    &-& \sum_{\Gamma \in \mathcal{E}} \int_{\Gamma} \rpth{\ave{\mathbf{q}^{(n,m)}c^{(n,m)}} - \frac{\lambda^{(n,m)}}{2}\jump{\zeta^{(n,m)}c^{(n,m)}}} \cdot \jump{\psi_{i}} \mathrm{d}\Sigma. 
    \label{eq:r_concentration}
\end{eqnarray}
\end{subequations}

The previously described discretization can be extended seamlessly to non-conforming, locally refined meshes, thanks to the flexible implementation of such meshes available in \texttt{deal.II}. These capabilities were already successfully exploited in \cite{orlando:2023b, orlando:2023a, orlando:2024} for the discretization of the Euler equations. Here, we follow the same approach as in these works. For the adaptive simulations, we denote the case-dependent refinement indicator on each element by $\eta_{K} > 0$. We refine and coarsen an element based on the following criteria:
%
\begin{eqnarray}
    \textrm{if   }& \eta_{K} > \theta_{r}\,\eta_{max}
    \textrm{ and } n_K < n_{max}
    \textrm{ and } \mathcal{H}_K > \mathcal{H}_{min} &\quad\textrm{refine } K \nonumber \\
    \textrm{if   }& \eta_{K} < \theta_{c}\,\eta_{max} & \quad \textrm{coarsen } K \nonumber 
\end{eqnarray}
%
where
%
$$\eta_{max} = \max_{\mathcal{K\in T_{H}}}\eta_{K},$$
%
whereas $\theta_{r}$ and $\theta_{c}$ are the refinement and coarsening thresholds, respectively. We denote by $n_{K}$ the mesh level of the element, ranging from the coarsest level of the initial mesh, $n_{K} = 1$, to the finest level $n_{K} = n_{max}$. For initial meshes with a variable mesh size, we also set a minimum target mesh size $\mathcal{H}_{min}$, below which a cell cannot be refined, even if $n_{K} < n_{max}$. Notice that, as discussed, e.g., in \cite{arpaia:2018, donat:2014, liang:2015, popinet:2011}, performing
dynamical mesh adaptation with non conforming meshes may lead to violations of either the {\cal C}-property or mass conservation. The approach proposed here does not guarantee mass conservation in the dynamical refinement case, while its well-balancing and {\cal C}-property remain unaffected, due to the choice of $\zeta$ as a prognostic variable. On the other hand, the method is both conservative and well balanced on statically adaptive meshes. In Section \ref{sec:realistic} we control the relative mass conservation error for a realistic adaptive coastal application.

Equations \eqref{eq:shw} do not include physical viscosity nor diffusion beyond the friction term and all numerical
experiments in Sections \ref{sec:tests} and \ref{sec:realistic} have been carried out without adding numerical viscosity or diffusion. However, for the realistic tests in Section \ref{sec:parallel}, a small numerical diffusion term was added to the concentration equation, as a simple monotonization mechanism. This is necessary to control spurious oscillations that arise in regions with large tracer gradients. This numerical dissipation was included adding to \eqref{eq:r_concentration} the term:
%
\begin{equation}\label{eq:numdiff}
    -\sum_{K \in \mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{H}}} \int_{K} \nu_{c}h^{(n,m)} \grad c^{(n,m)} \cdot \grad\psi_{i} \mathrm{d}\Omega,
\end{equation}
%
where $\nu_{c}$ is a constant eddy diffusivity scaled by the polynomial degree $r$ and by the level of refinement, i.e. $\nu_{c} = C \rpth{2^{(n_{max}-1)} r}^{-2}$, and the constant $C$ is to be
chosen empirically for each case.

\subsection{Properties of the spatial discretization}
\label{ssec:spacedisc_prop}

Although most of the DG approximation outlined above is relatively standard, we discuss here in detail some of its specific and appealing properties for simulations of coastal flows in realistic domains. Firstly, the treatment of the non-conservative pressure term is based on a double integration by parts, as originally proposed in \cite{bassi:1997}. More specifically, as in \cite{tumolo:2015}, we have:
%
\begin{eqnarray}\label{eq:pressure_tumolo}
    \sum_{K \in \mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{H}}} \int_{K} ghD_{h}\zeta \cdot \boldsymbol{\varphi}_{i} \mathrm{d}\Omega &=& \sum_{K \in \mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{H}}} \int_{K} gh\grad\zeta \cdot \boldsymbol{\varphi}_{i} \mathrm{d}\Omega  \\
    &+& \sum_{\Gamma \in \mathcal{E}} \int_{\Gamma} g\ave{h}\ave{\zeta}\jump{\boldsymbol{\varphi}_{i}} \mathrm{d}\Sigma - \sum_{\Gamma \in \mathcal{E}} \int_{\Gamma} g\ave{h}\jump{\zeta\boldsymbol{\varphi}_{i}} \mathrm{d}\Sigma \nonumber \\
    &=&\sum_{K \in \mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{H}}} \int_{K} gh\grad\zeta \cdot \boldsymbol{\varphi}_{i}d\Omega - \sum_{\Gamma \in \mathcal{E}} \int_{\Gamma} g\ave{h}\ave{\boldsymbol{\varphi}_{i}} \cdot \jump{\zeta} \mathrm{d}\Sigma,
\nonumber
\end{eqnarray}
%
where in the last relation we have employed the so-called \textit{magic formula} \cite{arnold:1982}:
%
\begin{equation}\label{eq:magic_formula}
    \jump{\zeta\boldsymbol{\varphi}_{i}} = \ave{\zeta}\jump{\boldsymbol{\varphi}_{i}} + \jump{\zeta} \cdot \ave{\boldsymbol{\varphi}_{i}}.
\end{equation}
%
For a continuous bathymetry, formulation \eqref{eq:pressure_tumolo} can be reinterpreted as a DG approximation of the pressure term written in flux form under the so-called strong form, which simply stands for a double integration by parts \cite{arpaia:2022, bonev:2018}. More specifically, starting from the conservative formulation of the pressure gradient and considering a centered flux for the pressure gradient, after a double integration by parts we get:
%
\begin{eqnarray}
    \sum_{K \in \mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{H}}} \int_{K} \rpth{D_{h}\rpth{P\mathbf{I}} + ghD_{h}z_{b}} \cdot \boldsymbol{\varphi}_{i} \mathrm{d}\Omega &=& \sum_{K \in \mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{H}}} \int_{K} g h\grad\zeta \cdot \boldsymbol{\varphi}_{i} \mathrm{d}\Omega \nonumber \\
    &+& \sum_{\Gamma \in \mathcal{E}} \int_{\Gamma} \ave{P}\jump{\boldsymbol{\varphi}_{i}}d\Sigma - \sum_{\Gamma \in \mathcal{E}} \int_{\Gamma} \jump{P\boldsymbol{\varphi}_{i}} \mathrm{d}\Sigma \\
    &=& \sum_{K \in \mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{H}}} \int_{K} gh\grad\zeta \cdot \boldsymbol{\varphi}_{i} \mathrm{d}\Omega - \sum_{\Gamma \in \mathcal{E}} \int_{\Gamma} \jump{P} \cdot \ave{\boldsymbol{\varphi}_{i}} \mathrm{d}\Sigma, \nonumber
\end{eqnarray}
%
with $P = \frac{1}{2}gh^{2}$ denoting the shallow water pressure. Thanks to \eqref{eq:magic_formula}, in the case of continuous bathymetry, we obtain
%
$$\jump{P} = g\ave{h}\jump{h} = g\ave{h}\jump{\zeta},$$ 
%
so as to recover relation \eqref{eq:pressure_tumolo}. Formulation \eqref{eq:pressure_tumolo} is similar, but not identical to that later proposed in \cite{orlando:2023c}, which reads as follows:
%
\begin{eqnarray}\label{eq:pressure_orlando_tmp}
    \sum_{K \in \mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{H}}} \int_{K} ghD_{h}\zeta \cdot \boldsymbol{\varphi}_{i} \mathrm{d}\Omega &=& \sum_{K \in \mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{H}}} \int_{K} gh\grad\zeta \cdot \boldsymbol{\varphi}_{i} \mathrm{d}\Omega \\
    &+&\sum_{\Gamma \in \mathcal{E}} \int_{\Gamma} g\ave{h\zeta}\jump{\boldsymbol{\varphi}_{i}} \mathrm{d}\Sigma - \sum_{\Gamma \in \mathcal{E}} \int_{\Gamma} g\ave{h}\jump{\zeta\boldsymbol{\varphi}_{i}} \mathrm{d}\Sigma.
    \nonumber 
\end{eqnarray}
%
After some algebraic manipulations, one obtains
%
\begin{equation}
    \ave{h\zeta} = \ave{h}\ave{\zeta} + \frac{1}{4}\jump{h} \cdot \jump{\zeta},
\end{equation}    
%
so that \eqref{eq:pressure_orlando_tmp} reduces to
%
\begin{eqnarray}\label{eq:pressure_orlando}
    \sum_{K \in \mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{H}}} \int_{K} ghD_{h}\zeta \cdot \boldsymbol{\varphi}_{i} \mathrm{d}\Omega &=& \sum_{K \in \mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{H}}} \int_{K} gh\grad\zeta \cdot \boldsymbol{\varphi}_{i} \mathrm{d}\Omega \nonumber \\
    &+&\sum_{\Gamma \in \mathcal{E}} \int_{\Gamma} g\rpth{\frac{1}{4}\jump{h}\jump{\boldsymbol{\varphi}_{i}} - \ave{h}\ave{\boldsymbol{\varphi}_{i}}} \cdot \jump{\zeta}d\Sigma.
\end{eqnarray}
%
We have inspected numerically the difference between the two formulations in the idealized tests, and no noticeable differences have been found. One can easily notice that both relation \eqref{eq:pressure_tumolo} and relation \eqref{eq:pressure_orlando} satisfy the {\cal C}-property exactly, without any constraint either on the quadrature formula or on the regularity of the bathymetry. Indeed, independently of $z_{b}$, one has $\grad\zeta = 0$ in $K$ and $\jump{\zeta} = 0$ on $\Gamma$ if $\zeta = const$.

The choice of the numerical quadrature formula is related to the need to integrate accurately the nonlinear advection term, which consist of non-polynomial terms. For integrals over the inter-element boundaries $\Gamma$ we use a Gauss-Legendre formula with $\textrm{floor}(\frac{3}{2}r) + 1$ quadrature points, whose degree of exactness is then $3r$. The same formula is applied to integrals over elements $K$ by considering tensor products of the quadrature points over the reference quadrilateral. Only for the mass matrix integrals we use a Gauss-Legendre quadrature formula with $r + 1$ points along each coordinate direction. Notice that, since the entries of the mass matrix \eqref{eq:mass_matrix} consist of the product of two basis functions, no aliasing error is introduced. The use of the same number of quadrature points and degrees of freedom along each coordinate direction yields, after a change of basis, diagonal blocks in the algebraic structure of the mass matrix \cite{kronbichler:2019}. More specifically, as explained in \cite{kronbichler:2019, kronbichler:2016}, the mass matrix is inverted as $\mathbf{M}^{-1} = \mathbf{S}^{-T}\mathbf{J}^{-1}\mathbf{S}^{-1}$. Here $\mathbf{J}$ is a diagonal matrix, whose entries are equal to the determinant of the Jacobian times the quadrature weight, while $\mathbf{S}$ is a square matrix with basis functions in the row and quadrature points in columns, i.e. $S_{ij} = \psi_{i}(\mathbf{x}_{j})$. The matrix $\mathbf{S}$ is constructed as the Kronecker product (tensor product) of small one-dimensional matrices that can be inverted efficiently at each stage. The same approach is applied to the modified mass matrices \eqref{eq:mass_matrix_discharge} and \eqref{eq:mass_matrix_concentration}. Notice that, if friction is present and for a general bathymetry, the entries can be no longer represented in general as the product of polynomials.

Another important theoretical property that is easy to verify for the proposed discretization is the consistency of the discrete forms of the concentration equation and continuity equation. As remarked in \cite{gross:2002}, this property is essential to guarantee monotonic solution for tracer transport coupled to free-surface flow, whenever flux form discretizations are employed. Inserting a constant concentration, into the concentration equation \ref{eq:algebraic_concentration} and comparing \eqref{eq:mass_rhs} and \eqref{eq:r_concentration}, one obtains
%
$$R^{(n,m)}_{i} = F^{(n,m)}_{i}.$$
%
Furthermore, one has
%
\begin{equation}\label{eq:cwc1}
    \sum_{K \in \mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{H}}} \int_{K} h^{(n,l)}\psi_{i} \mathrm{d}\Omega
    = \sum_{K \in \mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{H}}} \int_{K} h^{n}\psi_{i} \mathrm{d}\Omega +
    \Delta t \sum_{m=1}^{l-1}a_{lm} F^{(n,m)}_{i}.
\end{equation}
%
Using the definition of the water depth, one obtains
%
\begin{equation}\label{eq:cwc2}
    \sum_{K \in \mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{H}}} \int_{K} \rpth{\zeta^{(n,l)} + z_{b}}\psi_{i}d\Omega
    = \sum_{K \in \mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{H}}} \int_{K} \rpth{\zeta^{n}+z_{b}}\psi_{i} \mathrm{d}\Omega +
    \Delta t \sum_{m=1}^{l-1}a_{lm} F^{(n,m)}_{i}.
    %
\end{equation}
%
The integrals in \eqref{eq:cwc2} are computed by numerical integration, in which only the nodal values of the (in general) non-polynomial function $z_{b}$ are involved. These terms appear in an identical fashion on the left and right-hand side, so after cancellation and expansion of the polynomial expression of the free-surface in terms of basis functions we obtain
%
\begin{equation}\label{eq:cwc3}
    \sum_{j = 1}^{\left|\mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{H}}\right|(r+1)^{d}}\sum_{K \in \mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{H}}} \int_{K} \psi_{j}\psi_{i} \mathrm{d}\Omega \, \zeta^{(n,l)}_{j} = \sum_{j = 1}^{\left|\mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{H}}\right|(r+1)^{d}} \sum_{K \in \mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{H}}} \int_{K} \psi_{j}\psi_{i} \mathrm{d}\Omega \, \zeta^{n}_{j} + \Delta t \sum_{m=1}^{l-1}a_{lm} F^{(n,m)}_i,
\end{equation}
%
which is identical to the discrete continuity equation \eqref{eq:algebraic_elevation}. If instead the vertically integrated concentration $m = ch$ had been chosen as prognostic variable to be expanded in terms of the local polynomial basis, this would have entailed the need to compute, in $R^{(n,m)}_i$, the quotients $m/h$ to recover the concentration variables. Due to the non-polynomial nature of $h$ in our formulation, this would lead to the impossibility to guarantee theoretically the consistency with the fully discrete formula \eqref{eq:algebraic_elevation}, resulting in spurious monotonicity violations.

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%% Numerical tests %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{Idealized numerical experiments}
\label{sec:tests}

The numerical method outlined in Section \ref{sec:numerical} is now validated in a number of relevant idealized benchmarks. In the numerical experiments without friction, we test different fully explicit time discretizations beyond the three-stage second-order Runge-Kutta (RK32), which is the explicit companion method of the IMEX-RK method (see Appendix \ref{app:IMEX_coeffs}). More specifically, we employ the third order Strong Stability Preserving schemes \cite{gottlieb:2001}, denoted as SSP33, and the classical fourth order Runge-Kutta scheme (RK44), see, e.g., \cite{lambert:1991}. The time discretization is chosen to match the formal order of accuracy of the spatial discretization.

If an exact solution is available, accuracy is measured by the $L^{2}$ error norm on the free-surface and of the discharge, which we compute as
%
\begin{equation}\label{eq:errors_l2}
    \left|\left|\zeta - \zeta_{ex}\right|\right|^{2}_{2} = \sum_{K \in \mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{H}}} \int_{K} \rpth{\zeta - \zeta_{ex}}^{2} \mathrm{d}\Omega \qquad \left|\left|\mathbf{q} - \mathbf{q}_{ex}\right|\right|^{2}_{2} = \sum_{K \in \mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{H}}} \int_{K} \left|\mathbf{q} - \mathbf{q}_{ex}\right|^{2} \mathrm{d}\Omega.
\end{equation}

\subsection{Travelling vortex}
\label{ssec:travelling_vortex}

The first test case consists of a compact support travelling vortex which fulfils the shallow water equations with zero force term on the right hand side. For this test case, described in detail in \cite{ricchiuto:2021}, an analytical solution is available, which is used to assess the accuracy and correctness of the AMR implementation. The initial conditions read as follows:
%
\begin{eqnarray*}
    h(r) &=& h_{0} - \frac{4}{g}\rpth{\frac{4\Gamma r_{0}}{\pi}}^{2}\rpth{H\rpth{\pi/2} - H\rpth{\rho/2}},\quad \rho = \frac{\pi r}{r_{0}} \\
    %
    u &=& u_{\infty} - \rpth{y - y_{0}}\omega(\rho) \\
    v &=& \rpth{x - x_{0}}\omega(\rho),
\end{eqnarray*}
%
with $r$ denoting the distance from the vortex center $\rpth{x_{0}, y_{0}}$. Moreover, $u_{\infty}$ is the background velocity oriented along the $x-$axis at which the vortex is transported and
%
$$\omega(\rho) = 4\Gamma\cos^{4}\rpth{\rho/2},$$
%
where $\Gamma$ is a free parameter that controls the vortex strength. It has been selected to have a minimal depth of $h(0) = h_{min}$ at the center of the vortex. The definition of $H(x)$ is the following:
%
\begin{eqnarray*}
    H(x) &=& \frac{35\cos(2x)}{384} +\frac{35x\sin(2x)}{192} + \cos^{6}(x)\rpth{\frac{\cos^{2}(x)}{64} + \frac{7}{288}} + \frac{35\cos^{2}(2x)}{3072} + \frac{35x^{2}}{256} \\
    &+& \frac{35x\cos(2x)\sin(2x)}{768} + \frac{x\cos^{5}(x)\sin x\rpth{\cos^{2} x + \frac{7}{6}}}{8}
\end{eqnarray*}
%
Notice that the initial profiles and the solution are $\mathcal{C}^{4}$ functions, which is enough to test a fourth order accurate RK-DG scheme, according to the classical interpolation estimate of the finite element theory. The parameter values defining the vortex and the background flow are the following:
%
\begin{equation*}
    h_{0} = \SI{1}{\meter}, \quad h_{min} = \SI{0.9}{\meter}, \quad u_{\infty} = \SI{6}{\meter\per\second}, \quad r_{0} = \SI{0.25}{\meter} \quad \textrm{and} \quad \, \rpth{x_{0}, y_{0}} = \rpth{\SI{0.5}{\meter}, \SI{0.5}{\meter}}.
\end{equation*}
%
The final time is $T = \SI[parse-numbers=false]{1/6}{\second}$, while the computational domain is given by the rectangle $\rpth{0,\SI{2}{\meter}} \times \rpth{0,\SI{1}{\meter}}.$ Given the supercritical flow regime, the exact solution is imposed at the left inflow boundary, while a supercritical outflow is considered at the right boundary, where the internal value is imposed. A wall boundary condition is imposed at the top and bottom boundaries.

This benchmark is used to validate both the static and $h$-adaptive versions of the code. For the static simulations, the spatial domain is discretized with a Cartesian mesh with $2N \times N$ elements, where $N$ ranges from $N = 20$ at the coarsest mesh level to $N = 360$ for the finest one. For the adaptive simulations, the initial mesh corresponds to the coarsest mesh level with $N = 20$ and adaptation is switched on with the following parameters. The maximum refinement level ranges from $n_{max} = 2$, just one level beyond the coarsest static mesh, to $n_{max} = 5$, that reaches the resolution of the finest static mesh. The refinement indicator is taken as the absolute value of the vorticity, which should be a good estimator to detect vortex-like structures
%
\begin{equation}
    \eta_{K} = \max_{q \in K}\left|\nabla \times \vel(\mathbf{x}_{q})\right|,
\end{equation}
%
where $q$ is the index of the set of the quadrature points of the Gaussian formula on the element. The thresholds for coarsening and refining are $\theta_{c} = 5 \times 10^{-5}$ and $\theta_{r} = 10^{-4}$, respectively, whereas the remeshing procedure is performed every 5 time steps. These choices allow to properly refine the vortex core within a radius $r < r_{0}$ (Figure \ref{fig:vortex_mesh}). In Figure \ref{fig:vortex_metric} we compare the adaptive and static results with three different metrics presented in \cite{beisiegel:2021}: the ratio error over mesh size, the ratio error over number of degrees of freedom and the ratio of time over error. At all polynomial orders, the adaptive results show the same accuracy level as the static ones while using a much smaller number of degrees of freedom, which results in a significant reduction of the computational time. In particular, the adaptive simulations reaching the finest refinement level use from six to ten times less degrees of freedom and run from seven to nine times faster (Table \ref{tab:vortex_metric}). The total overhead of the AMR operations, including the computation of the refinement indicator, the refinement and coarsening operations, and the transfer of the solutions onto the new mesh, is between 23\% of the total wall time for the case $r = 1$ and 10\% for the case $r = 3$. 

