\subsection{Preservation of hydrostatic equilibrium}
\label{ssec:lake_at_rest_pert}

In order to check numerically the preservation of the hydrostatic equilibrium, or {\cal C}-property, that was discussed in Section \ref{sec:numerical}, we consider the classical lake-at-rest benchmark. A rectangular basin is considered with horizontal dimensions $\SI{2}{\meter} \times \SI{1}{\meter}$ and constant free-surface height value of $\SI{1}{\meter}$. The bathymetry profile is given by the non-smooth function
%
\begin{equation*}
    z_{b}(x,y) = -z_{b0}\exp\rpth{\psi(x,y)},
\end{equation*}
%
with
%
\begin{equation*}
    z_{b0} = \SI{0.65}{\meter}, \quad
    \psi = 
    \begin{cases}
        \sqrt{\rpth{x - 0.9}^{2} + \rpth{x - 0.5}^{2}}, & \text{if } \mathbf{x} \in \rpth{0.9,1.1} \times \rpth{0.3, 0.7} \\
        %
        -5\rpth{x - 0.9}^{2} - 50\rpth{x - 0.5}^{2}, & \text{otherwise,}
    \end{cases}
\end{equation*}
%
where all the length values given above are in meters. The mesh has $40 \times 20$ elements arranged in a Cartesian pattern. The elements have randomly distorted edges in correspondence of the discontinuity (Figure \ref{fig:lake_at_rest_pert_mesh}). As a result, the jump in the bathymetry is not aligned with the edges, but it crosses the elements and the edges themselves. In Table \ref{tab:lake_at_rest_pert_rest} we report the errors at $T = \SI{48}{\second}$, which corresponds to 10000-45000 time steps, depending on the polynomial order. It can be observed that the hydrostatic balance is preserved up to machine precision without any need of special treatment of the fluxes or the bathymetry.

Next, we consider a small amplitude perturbation of the lake-at-rest free-surface. The perturbation is confined in a narrow band:
%
\begin{equation*}
    \zeta(\mathbf{x},0)= 1 +
    \begin{cases}
        \SI{0.01}{\meter}, & \text{if } \SI{0.05}{\meter} \leq x \leq \SI{0.15}{\meter} \\
        0,    & \text{otherwise}
    \end{cases}
\end{equation*}
%
The perturbation generates two small waves. The left-propagating wave leaves the domain from the left boundary, thanks to a non-reflecting boundary condition, while the right-propagating one travels over the irregular bathymetry and is deformed as a consequence. We test the ability of the numerical scheme to handle robustly the irregular bathymetry by observing that small amplitude wave patterns are not polluted by spurious oscillations generated at the bathymetry jump. A reference solution is computed on a very fine regular mesh composed of $2000 \times 1000 = 2000000$ points with a robust, oscillation-free Finite Volume scheme. The FV scheme scheme is obtained from the numerical method of Section \ref{sec:numerical} with the choices of piecewise constant polynomials ($r = 0$) and forward Euler time stepping.

For this test, the final time is instead $T = \SI{0.48}{\second}$. We observe that, using the RK32 method for time discretizations, qualitatively correct results are obtained at all orders of accuracy (Figure \ref{fig:lake_at_rest_pert_contour}). As the polynomial order is increased, a richer wave pattern appears with better resolved wave fronts, peaks, and throats. With $r = 3$ the solution is close to the reference one, except for a train of small amplitude oscillations ahead of the leading wave. We have also run the same test using a polynomial representation of the bathymetry, obtained either by interpolation into or projection onto the finite element space used by the discretization. For $r \ge 2$, the bathymetry exhibits an oscillating behaviour with spurious fluctuations at the quadrature points that have a direct impact on the residual computation (Figure \ref{fig:lake_at_rest_pert_gibbs}). These oscillations are large enough to yield negative depths and none of these numerical experiments performed with these polynomial representations of the same bathymetry ends correctly.

