The first case is similar to the test proposed in \cite{rosatti:2011}, except for the use of a more regular bathymetry. The second case has a much larger value of the Manning friction coefficient, thus allowing to test the performance of the implicit treatment of the friction term in the IMEX time discretization method. In Figure \ref{fig:channel_rosatti} and \ref{fig:channel_superviscous}, we compare the numerical solution against the exact one obtained on the mesh with $40$ elements and $r = 1$. The convergence study, also in the same Figures, shows the correct empirical order of convergence for $r = 1,2,3$. Notice that, since this is a steady state benchmark, the spatial convergence order is always retrieved, in spite of the second order accuracy of the time discretization method. In the case with strong friction, on the coarsest meshes the simulations run stably with a time step that is approximately 2.5 times larger than the maximum time step for which the RK32 method could run stably in the same test.

A further channel flow test uses instead the following bathymetry profile:
%
\begin{eqnarray*}
    z_{b}(x) = 4.9 + 0.001 x &-& \frac{z_{b0}}{2} \rpth{\tanh\rpth{\frac{x - 0.4L}{c_{0}}} - \tanh\rpth{\frac{x - 0.5L}{c_{0}}}} \\
    &-& \frac{z_{b0}}{4}\rpth{\tanh\rpth{\frac{x - 0.55L}{c_{0}}} - \tanh\rpth{\frac{x - 0.6L}{c_{0}}}}. 
\end{eqnarray*}
%
This profile displays two obstacles with steep slopes. At the coarsest mesh resolution, with $10$ elements, these obstacles are unresolved at the grid scale. At the opposite, the finest resolution with $160$ elements captures the bathymetry profile. In Figure \ref{fig:channel_irregular}, we compare the numerical solution against the exact one, obtained on the mesh with $10$ elements and $r = 3$. The computed solution is close to the exact one, in spite of the undersampling of the bathymetry at the grid scale. We have also repeated the same test using a polynomial approximation of the bathymetry. The bathymetry coefficients at the nodes can be the interpolating ones or can be computed from a projection approach, the last being more suited for irregular data. In both cases, we get much larger errors with respect to our approach for the treatment of irregular bathymetries and the velocities past the hills are essentially wrong (Figure \ref{fig:channel_irregular} and Table \ref{tab:channel_irregular_errors}). This result highlights the advantage of using high-order polynomials to approximate the free-surface and a high-order quadrature formula that recovers accurate information on the sub-grid bathymetric features.

