\section{A benchmark with realistic bathymetry}
\label{sec:realistic}

We now consider a benchmark with more realistic bathymetry and complex geometry. More specifically, we consider the Venice Lagoon, a very complex sea water basin whose area is about $\SI{50}{\kilo\meter\squared}$ and which includes several interconnected narrow channels with a maximum width of $\SI{1}{\kilo\meter}$ and depth of $\SI{50}{\meter}$ surrounding large and flat shallow areas. The Lagoon is connected to the Adriatic Sea through three narrow inlets, namely Lido, Malamocco, and Chioggia (see Figure \ref{fig:veniceLagoon0}). The city of Venice is situated upon the largest island near Lido inlet. Tides propagate from the Adriatic Sea into the Lagoon through the three inlets. In order to assess the effectiveness and the accuracy of the non-conforming mesh refinement capabilities available in \texttt{deal.II}, we have considered both a conforming and a non-conforming mesh (Figure \ref{fig:mesh}). For the conforming case we consider a coarse mesh with $14,214$ elements with the edges aligned to the main channels. The resolution varies from $\SI{2}{\km}$ at open sea to an along-channel resolution of
$\SI{400}{\meter}$ at the inlets and close to the main channels and $\SI{200}{\meter}$ to follow narrow and irregular channels. The non-conforming mesh has been obtained with one further level of refinement, so that $n_{max} = 2$, assuming a minimum target mesh size $\mathcal{H}_{min} = \SI{100}{\meter}$ and a refinement indicator based on the bathymetry:
%
\begin{equation}
    \eta_{K} = \max_{q \in K} z_{b}(\mathbf{x}_{q}),
\end{equation}
%
where $q$ denotes the quadrature points of the Gaussian formula on the element. The thresholds for coarsening and refining are $\theta_{c}\eta_{max} = \theta_{r}\eta_{max} = \SI{4}{\meter}$. These choices allow to refine the main channels of the lagoon.

For both meshes, we have considered the same high resolution bathymetry database employed in \cite{bajo:2021,ferrarin:2010}, performing linear interpolation within each pixel of dimension $\SI{15}{\meter}\times\SI{15}{\meter}$, to recover the bathymetry data needed at the quadrature points. In order to avoid the need for a wetting and drying algorithm, the bathymetry has been set to the constant value $\SI{1}{\meter} $ in the shallow areas with   $z_{b} \leq \SI{1}{\meter}$. The only dissipation mechanism is the bottom friction, for which we set a uniform Manning coefficient $n = \SI{0.055}{\meter^{-1/3}\second}$. Given this setting with complex geometry and realistic bathymetry, we consider a semi-realistic test in which an M2 tide with amplitude of $\SI{0.3}{\meter}$ and period of $\SI{12.41}{\hour}$ has been specified at the Adriatic Sea boundary. Zero wind stress and zero mass flux at the internal boundaries of the Lagoon are assumed. 

We run a simulation with the non-conforming mesh with $r = 1$ and another one with the conforming mesh with $r = 2$. The two simulations use roughly the same number of degrees of freedom and can therefore be compared. We have also run a simulation with the conforming mesh and $r = 3$. For further comparison, a numerical solution has been computed using the SHYFEM model \cite{umgiesser:2004} on a very fine mesh, much finer than the \texttt{deal.II} mesh, made up of $98,772$ triangular elements that resolve most of the lagoon channels, up to a channel width of $\SI{25}{\meter}$. Such system is run by the CNR-ISMAR as an operational model to forecasts the water level in Venice \cite{bajo:2021, ferrarin:2010}. Table \ref{tab:veniceLagoon} summarizes all the parameter values defining the simulations.

In Figure \ref{fig:veniceLagoon1} and \ref{fig:veniceLagoon2} we compare the free surface elevation at high and low tide. Our results are very similar to those of SHYFEM, with slightly larger amplitudes in the northern lagoon, in the order of a few $\SI{}{\cm}$. In Figure \ref{fig:veniceLagoon3} and \ref{fig:veniceLagoon4} we compare the circulations at flood and ebb tide. Also the velocities we obtain are similar to those of the SHYFEM model, with similar shapes of the circulation patterns and of the jets at the inlets. The circulation pattern seems slightly noisier with respect to SHYFEM. The reason could be the post-processing of the velocity from the prognostic variables, in presence of an irregular bathymetry, or the absence of an explicit diffusion term or of a slope limiting technique. 
The time series of the solution, recorded at specific points in correspondence of the three inlets and inside the lagoon, confirms these results (Figure \ref{fig:veniceLagoon5}).

