Next, we present results obtained coupling a tracer concentration equation to the shallow water flow. We have added the transport of a tracer to the previous realistic configuration of the Venice lagoon. Only for these tests, the numerical diffusion described in Equation \eqref{eq:numdiff} was added, setting $C = \SI{1}{\meter\squared\per\second}$ based on a sensitivity study.

In a first experiment, we check the consistency of the tracer discretization with the continuity property, discussed in Section \ref{sec:numerical}. The relative errors after one tidal cycle, shown in Table \ref{tab:veniceLagoon_tracer}, are close to machine accuracy. As discussed in \cite{gross:2002}, this ensures that no spurious violations of monotonicity will be produce in the simulation of tracer transport.

We have then simulated a semi-realistic configuration so as to test our dynamic AMR procedure based on non-conforming meshes. The tracer is initialized as $c = 1$ inside the lagoon or $c = 0.9$ outside, specifying at the sea boundary a tracer value equal to the initial one $c = 0.9$. The resulting tracer front at the inlets is transported by the tidal currents in and out from the lagoon. At each tidal cycle, lower tracer concentrations intrude into the lagoon through the main channels, while higher tracers values form a plume outside the inlets. Rather than performing an academic convergence test, we try to provide some qualitative arguments highlighting the advantages of our AMR technique. For this purpose, we run two simulations with the conforming mesh (labeled as coarse mesh) with polynomial orders $r=2$ and $r=3,$ respectively. The results with $r=2$ are not shown since, on these relatively coarse meshes, they do not differ substantially from the $r=3$ case. We then run an adaptive simulation with a dynamically adaptive non-conforming  mesh with $r=2$. The dynamic mesh has two levels of refinement beyond the coarse mesh, so that $n_{max} = 3$, assuming a minimum target mesh size $\mathcal{H}_{min} = \SI{50}{\meter}$. The refinement indicator is based on the gradient of the concentration
%
\begin{equation}
    \eta_{K} = \max_{q \in K} \nabla c(\mathbf{x}_{q}),
\end{equation}
%
where $q$ denotes the index of the set of the quadrature points of the Gaussian formula on each element. The thresholds for coarsening and refining are $\theta_{c} = 10^{-2}$ and $\theta_{r} = 5 \times 10^{-3}$. Notice that, during the refinement and coarsening process, the appropriate bathymetry values for the new resolution are extracted at run time from the bathymetry database. With these choices, we were able to track the tracer fronts with a variable mesh size between $\SI{100}{\meter}$ and $\SI{200}{\meter}$. Table \ref{tab:veniceLagoon_tracer0} summarizes all the parameter values defining the simulation meshes and the relative wall times.
From the comparison of Figures \ref{fig:veniceLagoon_tracer1} and \ref{fig:veniceLagoon_tracer3} it is apparent that AMR allows to compute sharper fronts and filaments and to resolve small-scale structures that are absent in the static runs. One can observe for example the two counterrotating vortices inside the Malamocco inlet or the vortex wake past to the Malamocco southern dyke (Figure \ref{fig:veniceLagoon_tracer4}). Since the adaptive simulation violates the mass conservation statement, we have inspected the relative mass error. The time history of the relative mass error (not shown) exhibits a very small relative loss/gain in the total mass, on the order of $10^{-6}$.

