\section{Parallel efficiency}
\label{sec:parallel}

Finally, we have assessed the parallel performance of our adaptive model in a strong scaling test. The test is based on the travelling vortex benchmark described in Section \ref{ssec:travelling_vortex}. We keep constant the size of the problem, or the total number of degrees of freedom, and increase the number of processors used for the computation. We have pushed the level of refinement to $n_{max} = 7$ for the static simulation and $n_{max} = 8$ for the adaptive simulation. With a polynomial of degree $r = 3$, this corresponds to 157,286,400 degrees of freedom for the static simulation and 61,160,640 for the adaptive simulation. All the other parameters are the same as in Section \ref{ssec:travelling_vortex}, included the final time. Figure \ref{fig:parallel_0} shows the measured speed-up on a bilogarithmic scale, which is computed assuming that the wall time for 1 processor is 48 times slower than the wall time for 48 processors. The tests were performed on the Galileo100 machine at the CINECA computing centre. The nodes used for the computations are  x86 Intel Xeon Platinum 8276/L 2.4GHz bi-processors with 384 GB RAM. Each node consists of 48 processors. The measured scalability is very satisfying, with an efficiency of 82.8\% and 87.6\% for the static and the adaptive run, respectively, up to 1536 processors.

