%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%% Conclusions %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{Conclusions}
\label{sec:conclu}

We have proposed and validated a high-order Discontinuous Galerkin (DG) solver for coastal engineering applications, based on the \texttt{deal.II} library. The library capabilities have been exploited to handle automatically non-conforming meshes and implement both static and dynamic AMR approaches. In this work, only discretization methods have been employed that treat the free-surface gradients in an explicit way, as a first step towards the application of advanced and high-order IMEX techniques. Thanks to the use of the free surface as prognostic variable, which is well justified for the foreseen application regimes, the proposed method is automatically well-balanced, allows the use of realistic bathymetry data without any regularity assumption, and includes a consistent conservative discretization for transported chemical species. More specifically, since the bathymetry values are only evaluated at the quadrature points, the method allows to employ realistic bathymetric datasets without need of any smoothing or pre-processing.

We have performed a wide range of numerical experiments on idealized benchmarks, showing the effectiveness of the AMR approaches in maintaining a high level of accuracy while reducing the computational cost. Results obtained on realistic bathymetries and complex domains show the potential of the method for accurate and efficient adaptive simulations of coastal flows. The good parallel performance of the AMR implementation has also been demonstrated in a strong scaling test.

In future work, we aim to extend the present approach by a mass-conserving treatment of wetting and drying based on the proposal in \cite{casulli:2009}, also addressing the mass conservation issues for dynamically adapted meshes. We then plan to implement efficient Implicit-Explicit Runge-Kutta (IMEX-RK) time discretization schemes with an implicit treatment of the pressure gradient terms, as already done in \cite{orlando:2022, orlando:2023a} for atmospheric models, thus guaranteeing time step restrictions based on velocity rather than celerity values. Finally, we plan to include basic eddy viscosity turbulence models and to proceed to validate the resulting adaptive coastal flow model on completely realistic benchmarks.

