If we are dealing with trees that have height equal to $2$, then the distance can be computed analyzing the swap distance between the corresponding permutations, 
whose time complexity is linear, once the swap distance can be computed in linear time as well. 
Otherwise, the distances between trees is obtained by the recursive formula in the proof of \autoref{thm:distance}, which can be performed in $O(n^3)$ running time. 



\section{Consensus problems}\label{sec:closest}

The relationship between swap distances and trees of height $2$ implies that the knowledge of some consensus problems can be stated directly when asking about trees. 
Popov~\cite{popov2007multiple} showed that {\sc Swap-Closest Problem} is \NP-complete. Since each swap either breaks or merges cycles, a swap can be viewed as an operation on a tree of height $2$. 
Hence, a permutation is a solution of {\sc Swap-Closest} problem if and only if its cycle representation is a solution of the {\sc closest} problem in the Newick format, as a consequence of \autoref{thm:h2=swap}. Therefore, this implies \autoref{thm:closesth2}. 

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:closesth2}
Deciding the {\sc closest} problem of a set of trees is \NP-complete, even if the height of the input trees is equal to $2$.
\end{theorem}
% \begin{proof}
% Popov~\cite{popov2007multiple} showed that {\sc Swap-Closest Problem} is \NP-complete. A swap operation can be viewed as an operation on a tree of height $2$, since each swap either breaks or merges cycles. 
% Hence, a permutation is a solution of {\sc Swap-Closest Problem} if and only if its cycle representation is a solution of the {\sc closest} problem in Newick format, as a consequence of \autoref{thm:h2=swap}.
% \qed\end{proof}



Now, regarding the {\sc Median} problem we prove the following: 
the {\sc Median} problem of a set of trees is \NP-complete, even if there are three input trees. 
This restriction of the {\sc Median} problem is what we call by {\sc Tree-Median$_3$}. 
The hardness of {\sc Tree-Median$_3$} (\autoref{thm:TreeMedian3NPC}) is obtained by a reduction from the {\sc Breakpoint-Median$_3$} problem considering instances that do not contain two consecutive breakpoints, which is a restriction that we show the hardness in \autoref{thm:breakpointRestriction} of the general \NP-complete {\sc Breakpoint-Median$_3$} problem~\cite{pe1998median}. 
Based on that, we show in \autoref{thm:Closest3NPC} that the {\sc Closet} problem of a set of trees is \NP-complete, even if there are three input trees. 
This problem is what we call by {\sc Tree-Closest$_3$}. 


An \emph{adjacency of a permutation $\pi$ with respect to permutation~$\sigma$} is a pair $(\pi[i], \pi[i+1])$ of consecutive elements in $\pi$ such that this pair  
is also consecutive in $\sigma$, i.e., $\pi[i] = \sigma[j]$ and $\pi[i+1]=\sigma[j+1]$. 
If a pair of consecutive elements is not an adjacency, then it is called a \emph{breakpoint}, 
and we denote by $d_{\sf BP}(\pi, \sigma)$ the number of breakpoints of~$\pi$ with respect to~$\sigma$. 
The set $\adj(\pi)$ is the set of \emph{adjacencies of $\pi$}, given by $\adj(\pi) = \{\{\pi[i], \pi[i+1]\} \mid i=1, \ldots, n-1 \}$. 
Thus, in other words, the breakpoint distance between $\pi$ and $\sigma$ is $d_{\sf BP}(\pi, \sigma) = |\adj(\pi) - \adj(\sigma)|$. 


\begin{theorem}[$\star$]\label{thm:breakpointRestriction}
{\sc Breakpoint-Median$_3$} is \NP-complete, even for instances that do not contain two consecutive breakpoints. 
\end{theorem}
