\begin{theorem}\label{thm:TreeMedian3NPC}
{\sc Tree-Median$_3$} is \NP-complete, even for trees with height at most~$2$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
This is proved by a reduction from the {\sc Breakpoint-Median$_3$} problem restricted to instances that do not contain two consecutive breakpoints, which is \NP-complete, as proved in \autoref{thm:breakpointRestriction}. 
Let $\pi_1, \pi_2, \pi_3$ be an instance of {\sc Breakpoint-Median$_3$} satisfying the non-existence of two consecutive breakpoints. 
Assume, without loss of generality, that $T_1$ is a star tree associated to $\pi_1$, i.e. there is a unique internal node father of all leaves which are elements of $\pi_1$. 
Now, considering the breakpoints of $\pi_2$ with respect to $\pi_1$, 
we obtain the following tree  (see \autoref{fig:breakpoints} for an illustration): 

% \vspace{-.8cm}
\begin{figure}[!h]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=10cm]{breakpointTrees}
% \vspace{-.5cm}
    \caption{In the left there is a permutation $\pi_y$ with its corresponding tree $T_y$. In the right there is a permutation $\pi_x$ with its breakpoints with respect to $\pi_y$, along with its tree $T_{x,y}$. Bullets between $\pi_{x}[1]$ and $\pi_{x}[2]$, also between $\pi_{x}[3]$ and $\pi_{x}[4]$ represent breakpoints between $\pi_x$ and $\pi_y$. \label{fig:breakpoints}}
\end{figure}


Obtain a star tree rooted in a node $v$;  
For each breakpoint $a\ b$ of $\pi_2$ with respect to $\pi_1$, create 
a son $u$ of $v$ for which $u$ is father of leaves $a$ and $b$; 
Call such a tree as $T_{2,1}$. 
Next, apply the same transformation among the other pair of permutations, obtaining them $T_{3,1}$ and $T_{2,3}$. 
By construction, each of such trees has height at most $2$. 

Since each breakpoint of a permutation implies in a branch of a star tree containing the pair, and there are no two consecutive breakpoints among the permutations of the instance, 
then 
this implies that $d_{\sf BP}(\pi_x, \pi_y) = d(T_{x,y}, T_y)$, because for each breakpoint $a \ b$ we must ascend it by applying one to become sons of the root $v$ as in $T_y$, and neither $a$ nor $b$ forms another breakpoint with $x$ and $y$. 
Since the breakpoint distances between permutations remain the same when looking at the trees we just obtained, we have that {\sc Tree-Median$_3$} is also \NP-complete.
\qed\end{proof}

Now, we are able to relate {\sc median} and {\sc closest} problems concerning to tree distances, 
for which is not possible to be obtained considering other genome rearrangement operation~\cite{cunha2024complexity,cunha2020computational}. 



\begin{theorem}\label{thm:Closest3NPC}
{\sc Tree-Closest$_3$} is \NP-complete.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
This is done by a polynomial transformation from the {\sc Tree-Median$_3$} problem. 
Let $T_1, T_2$ and $T_3$ be trees of {\sc Tree-Median$_3$} with $n$ leaves each one. Hence all elements from~$1$ to $n$ appears in every tree. 
First, we show in \autoref{fact} an upper bound on the distance between two trees. 

\begin{fact}\label{fact}
Let $h$ be height of two trees $T_x$ and $T_y$ with $n$ leaves. Hence, $d(T_x, T_y) \leq 2nh$.
\end{fact}
\begin{proof}
In order to transform $T_x$ into $T_y$, for each leaf of $T_x$ in the worst case we should move it be be sun of the root $r_x$ and, after that, move it to become in a correct position as it is placed in $T_y$. 
Hence, we have applied at most $2h$ movements for each one of the $n$ elements.   
\qed\end{proof}

Now, we obtain a tree $T'_x$ (illustrated in \autoref{fig:path}(i)), for $x\in \{1,2,3\}$ as follows. 
Let $r_{x}$ be the root of $T_x$ with height $h_x$, then:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item Create a path graph with $2nh_x+1$ nodes, $p_x^1, \ldots, p_x^{2nh_x+1}$.

    \item For each node $u$ of the path graph we created below, except for the last one $p_x^{2nh_x+1}$, add a pendant node, i.e. a leaf node $u'$ whose father is $u$. 

    \item For each leaf ${p_{x}^{i}}'$, sun of a node $p_x^i$ we have created below, set its label as $i+n$, for $i = 1, \ldots, 2nh_x$. 

    \item Identify the nodes $p_x^{2nh_x+1}$ and $r_x$. 
\end{enumerate} 

% \vspace{-.5cm}
\begin{figure}[!h]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=9cm]{merge.pdf}
% \vspace{-.5cm}
    \caption{(i) Construction of $T'_x$ from the tree $T_x$ with $n$ leaves (with all elements from~$1$ to $n$) and height $h_x$. (ii) Tree obtained by the merge operation between $T'_x$ and $T'_y$, where $p_y^1$ is the rooted of the obtained tree $T'_{x,y}$.\label{fig:path}}
\end{figure}

Now, considering $T'_x$ and $T'_y$ trees obtained from the previous procedure, we apply a \emph{merge operation} of them into a unique tree $T'_{x,y}$ defined as follows (illustrated in \autoref{fig:path}(ii)). 
Let $p_x^1$ and $p_y^1$ be the roots of $T'_x$ and $T'_y$, respectively, then:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item For each leaf $i$ of $T'_{y}$, for $i = 1, \ldots, 2nh_y +1$, relabel it as $i+m_x$, where $m_x$ is the number of leaves of $T'_x$, i.e. $m_x = 2nh_x +1$. 

    \item Add an edge between $p_x^1$ and $p_y^1$ in such a way that $p_x^1$ is sun of $p_y^1$. 
\end{enumerate}

The resulted tree is $T'_{x,y}$, containing all elements from $1$ to $2n(h_x + h_y) +2$.

Now, based on a general instance of the {\sc Tree-Median$_3$} we build a particular instance of {\sc Tree-Closest$_3$} by a series of merge operations, and we prove an equivalence between the corresponding solutions, as described in \autoref{lm:medianiffclosest}. 


\begin{lemma}\label{lm:medianiffclosest}
Given three trees $T_1, T_2$ and $T_3$, the tree $T_\sigma$ is a solution of {\sc Tree-Median$_3$} if and only if the tree $T'_{\sigma,\sigma,\sigma}$ is a solution of {\sc Tree-Closest$_3$} for the trees 
$T'_{1,2,3}, T'_{2,3,1}$, and $T'_{3,1,2}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Since trees $T'_{1,2,3}, T'_{2,3,1}$, and $T'_{3,1,2}$ are composed by three parts of merge operations and, since for each $T_x$ we have added more than $2nh$ elements, based on \autoref{fact}, it is less costly to work with each part separately than to move a whole tree to take place another position. 
Hence, each part can be treated separately without loss of optimality, and then there is a solution of {\sc Tree-Closest$_3$} where all parts have the same solution $T'_\delta$. 
Therefore, there is a tree $x\in \{T'_{1,2,3}, T'_{2,3,1}, \allowbreak T'_{3,1,2}\}$ such that $d(T'_{\delta,\delta,\delta}, x) = (T'_\delta, T'_1)+ d(T'_\delta, T'_2)+ d(T'_\delta, T'_3)$. 
Since we want $T'_\delta$ such that $d(T'_{\delta,\delta,\delta}, x)$ is minimized, we want $\delta$ such that $d(T_\delta, T_1)+ d(T_\delta, T_2)+ d(T_\delta, T_3)$ is minimized. Hence, this happens if and only if $T_\delta = T_\sigma$, where $T_\sigma$ is solution of {\sc Tree-Median$_3$}.
\qed\end{proof}

Hence, \autoref{lm:medianiffclosest} concludes the proof.
\qed\end{proof}



Approaches considering \FPT (\emph{fixed parameterized tractable}) algorithms for {\sc Swap-closest} have been proposed in~\cite{cunha2024complexity}, such as the non-existence of polynomial kernels parameterized by the target distance $d$ and an $\bigO^*(d)^{\bigO(d)}$ running time algorithm, where $\bigO^*(f(n))$
is used to say that there exists an algorithm that runs in time
$\bigO(f(n))\cdot poly(n)$, where $poly(n)$ is a polynomial function in $n$. 
Since the equivalence between tree distances with height equal to $2$ and swap distances between the corresponding Newick format of the trees, these previous results considering \FPT approaches can also be stated for {\sc closest} problem with trees of height equal to $2$. 
Considering the {\sc median} problem, it is a long open problem the decision of {\sc swap median}, even when considering three input permutations. 
Based on that, polynomial kernel in~\cite{cunha2024complexity} was developed and can be adapted for both consensus problems. 
Initially, to compute distances between trees, we considered two strings in the Newick format to represent two trees as inputs. To study the {\sc Closest} and {\sc Median} problems, we developed two algorithms, which we describe next, to analyze more than two input trees. 

% \subparagraph{Generalizing the Maximum Common Almost $v$-tree}
\paragraph{Generalizing the Maximum Common Almost $v$-tree.}

For the first algorithm, the approach is to generalize the {\sc maximum common almost $v$-tree} problem defined in \autoref{sec:mcat} by now considering a set of $k\geq 2$ input trees. 
The main idea is to derive the MCAT of the set of $k$ input trees. 
This is done by contracting the MCAT solution $H$ in each input tree and simultaneously constructing the set $H$, part of the solution tree $T^*$, to the considered consensus problem. 
While the solution is not yet complete, this previous step continues with the remaining trees. 

A subtree $H'$ that we get in an MCAT step is settled to be at a position of $T^*$ as follows. 
Let $v$ be the most frequent ancestral node of $H'$ among the input trees: 
If $v$ is already in $T^*$, then $H'$ is settled to be a descendent of $v$ in $T^*$; 
If $v$ is not yet been included in $T^*$, we add $H'$ to an auxiliary set that will be included in $T^*$ as soon as $v$ is placed in that set. 
Finally, for each solution subtree $H'$ of an MCAT step, we set the values of its leaves in the solution tree $T^*$ to be in the same order as the most frequent order among the input subtrees~$H'$. 

We will now argue that the most frequent ancestral node of $H'$ among the input trees belongs to the solution tree $T^*$, and thus, such a node $v$ can be considered the ancestral node of $H'$ in $T^*$ (as described above). 
Assume that $H'$ is a solution of MCAT among all input trees. 
Furthermore, it is assumed that the most frequent ancestral node of $H'$ is $v$, but that $v$ does not belong to $T^*$. 
Since $v$ appears over the input trees, it can be concluded that $v$ is ancestral of a leaf that does not belong to $H'$. This leaf is denoted by $i$, with $i \in \{1, \ldots, n\}$. 
Since $i$ must belong to $T^*$, as it is a leaf, there must be a step of applying a MCAT over the remaining trees, after obtaining $H'$, that includes $i$ in a solution. 


% After the conclusion, 
We have obtained $T^*$ which is candidate solution tree for the {\sc Closest} and the {\sc Median} problems. 
This is achieving by taking the maximum distance between $T^*$ and all input trees and the sum of the distances between $T^*$ and the other trees, respectively. 


% \subparagraph{Phylogenetic tree}
\paragraph{Phylogenetic tree.}

A phylogenetic tree is a graphical representation, in the form of a tree, of the evolutionary relationships between biological entities, usually sequences or species~\cite{kapli2020phylogenetic}. 
Each leaf represents a distinct specie and each internal node of this tree represents the most common ancestor from all descendants from that point. 
Thus, the root node correspond to the most common ancestor of all the species leaves in the tree.

Based on this concept, the second algorithm takes as input $k$ trees, where $k$ is an arbitrary number, and try to replicate the same idea as the phylogenetic tree. 
Among all pairs of trees, select the one that is the closest pair between all. 
Let $k_1$ and $k_2$ be such a pair. 
After selecting both trees, replace it with another tree $j$ that belongs to a shortest path between $k_1$ and $k_2$, and $d(j,k_1) = d(j,k_2)$. 
The next step is to consider the tree $j$ instead of $k_1$ and $k_2$, and to select the closest pair among the reaming $k-1$ trees. 
This process causes the number of inputs to decrease by one each time, and repeating it $k-1$ times results in a singular tree, which is the candidate solution tree for the {\sc Closest} and {\sc Median} problems. 






