\section{Conclusions and Future Work}\label{sec:conclusions}

This study presents a comprehensive in-situ evaluation of mid-band deployments---enterprise Wi-Fi, MNO macro, and neutral-host networks---at a large big-box store, focusing on coverage, PHY-layer and application-layer throughput. Our measurements reveal that while Wi-Fi requires 65 APs to achieve extensive indoor coverage, the NH deployment provides comparable coverage with just six CBSDs and offers superior indoor coverage compared to MNO macro deployments. Analyzing PHY-layer performance, the NH network consistently outperforms the MNO macro indoors in both downlink and uplink, for both per-channel and aggregated throughput. Application-layer HTTP downlink throughput analysis further reinforces that the NH network provides a superior user experience compared to Wi-Fi and indoor MNO macro deployments, making it a strong candidate for future mid-band indoor connectivity solutions.

% This paper presented a comprehensive in-situ evaluation of three co-located wireless technologies---MNO macro, neutral-host offloaded MNO and enterprise Wi-Fi---within a large operational big-box retail environment. Our comparative analysis leveraged measurement dataset that captured a wide range of PHY-, MAC-, and application-layer parameters across cellular and Wi-Fi technologies. The analysis concludes that MNO macro networks provide the best performance in outdoor environments, with their effectiveness largely dependent on deployment density and the aggregation of extensive PHY-layer resources. A central focus of this study was to investigate whether these PHY resources—particularly those from outdoor deployments—could be leveraged in parallel for indoor use without causing detrimental inter-system interference. Our results show that, through the use of indoor neutral-host deployments operating in unlicensed shared spectrum bands (\ie CBRS), it is indeed possible to achieve indoor performance comparable to outdoor macro deployments, all while maintaining coexistence and avoiding performance degradation across systems.

% In addition to this, an important question remains: is the adoption of neutral-host networks justified, given the substantial deployment and standardization changes required, and does it offer performance benefits over already existing Wi-Fi technologies? Our comparative analysis reveals that enterprise Wi-Fi, operating in unlicensed spectrum, suffers from inherent inefficiencies due to its contention-based medium access. These inefficiencies---exacerbated in dense deployments, as shown in our analysis---necessitate meticulous tuning and coordination to ensure consistent performance. In contrast, our results demonstrate that the neutral-host deployment achieved superior throughput and coverage using significantly fewer PHY resources, thereby supporting the feasibility and potential benefits of adopting the neutral-host model for indoor connectivity.

