\paragraph{CT-Prefix} 
\looseness=-10000
We freeze $\Theta_{\mathrm{CT}}$, average the parameters across tokens to obtain an average prefix $\bar \Theta_{\mathrm{CT}}$, and then form a new trainable $m$-token prefix $\Theta_{\mathrm{prefix}}$ by adding small Gaussian perturbations:
\begin{equation*}
    \Theta_{\mathrm{prefix}} = \{\bar\Theta_{\mathrm{CT}} + \epsilon_i \}_{i=1}^m,
\end{equation*}
\looseness=-10000
where $\epsilon_i \in \mathcal{N}(0, 0.02)$. The model additionally conditions on $\Theta_{\mathrm{prefix}}$, analogous to Prefix Tuning. Since we only train $\Theta_{\mathrm{prefix}}$, this variant has the same number of trainable parameters as Prefix Tuning with $m$ tokens.

\looseness=-10000
We evaluate \ourmethodv{} and \ourmethodprefix{} across all benchmarks and compare them to \ourmethodkv{} in Table~\ref{tab:parameterefficient}, showing that both parameter-efficient variants retain most of the performance gain of \ourmethodkv{} and outperform Prefix Tuning. For \ourmethodv{}, we use the same hyperparameters as \ourmethodkv{} from Section~\ref{sec:hyperparams}. For \ourmethodprefix{}, we find that higher learning rates, 1e-1 for NLP-LR and 5e-2 for other datasets, are needed for better performance.

