Throughout this section, we fix a pure $d$-dimensional simplicial complex $X$ and a probability distribution $\mu$ on its facets $X(d)$ such that the support of $\mu$ is $X(d)$, i.e., $\mu(\sigma)>0$ for all facets $\sigma$.
In the rest of this section we define $\cC$-Lorentzian polynomials for commutative $\pi$ maps and prove a classification theorem: If $X$ is connected and all quadratic partial derivatives of $p$ are log-concave then $p$ is $\cC$-Lorentzian; see \cref{thm:conn+quad=Lor}.

