\begin{abstract}
%% Motivation
In the micro frontends architectural style, the frontend is divided into smaller
components, which can range from a simple button to an entire page. The goal is
to improve scalability, resilience, and team independence, albeit at the cost of
increased complexity and infrastructure demands. 
%% Objective
This paper seeks to provide insights into when the adoption of micro frontends 
may be worthwhile, particularly in an industry context, considering that research 
in this area is still evolving.
%% Methodology
To achieve this, we conducted an investigation into the state of the art of
micro frontends, based on both academic and gray literature.
We then implemented this architectural style in a marketplace for handcrafted
products, which already used microservices.
Finally, we evaluated the implementation through a semi-open questionnaire
with the developers.
%% Justification
At the studied marketplace company, the need for architectural change arose
due to the tight coupling between their main system (a Java monolith) and a
dedicated frontend system. Additionally, there were deprecated technologies
and poor developer experience.
%% Execution
To address these issues, the micro frontends architecture was adopted,
along with the \Pattern{API Gateway} and \Pattern{Backend for Frontend}
patterns, and technologies such as Svelte and Fastify.
%% Analysis
Although the adoption of Micro Frontends was successful, it was not strictly
necessary to meet the company's needs. According to the analysis of the mixed
questionnaire responses, other alternatives, such as a monolithic frontend,
could have achieved comparable results.
What made adopting micro frontends the most convenient choice in the company's
context was the monolith strangulation and microservices adoption, which
facilitated implementation through infrastructure reuse and knowledge sharing
between teams.

