\section{Liar's logic}\label{A: pseudocode}
The players of the \textit{Liar's dice} game need to decide at each turn whether they increment or challenge the previous bid. To do so, they follow the logic presented in Algorithm \ref{alg:liars_logic}.

\begin{algorithm}[h]
   \caption{The logic of the individual \textit{Liar's dice} player $i$}
   \label{alg:liars_logic}
\begin{algorithmic}
    \STATE {\bfseries Global variables:} The maximal number of pips on the dice $p_{max}$, the likelihood threshold $l = 0.3$.
   \STATE {\bfseries Input:} the number of pips $p_{t-1}$ and quantity $q_{t-1}$ of the previous bid $b_{t-1}$, the player's dice outcomes $d^i$,  the player's beliefs about the dice odds $\hat{\theta}^i_t$ and the player's bluff parameter $\lambda_t^i$.\\[3mm]
   \STATE $e \gets \text{estimate\_occurrences}(p_{t-1}, d^i, \hat{\theta}^i_t)$ \hfill \textcolor{gray}{// Estimate the number of occurrences of $p_{t-1}$.}

    \IF{$q_{t-1} > e$}
    \STATE $p_t \gets p_{t-1} + 1$ \hfill \textcolor{gray}{// Increase the number of pips for the new bid.}
    \IF{$p_t > p_{max}$}
    \STATE {\bfseries return } Challenge the previous bid
    \ENDIF
    \ELSE
    \STATE $p_t \gets p_{t-1}$
    \ENDIF\\[3mm]
    \IF{$\text{bid\_probability}(p_t, q_t, \hat{\theta}_t^i)< l$}
    \STATE {\bfseries return } Challenge the previous bid 
    \hfill \textcolor{gray}{// Challenge when the new bid seems unlikely.}
    \ELSE
    \STATE $q_t \gets q_t + \text{Poisson}(\lambda_t^i)$ \hfill \textcolor{gray}{// Add bluff.}
    \STATE {\bfseries return } $p_t$, $q_t$ \hfill \textcolor{gray}{// Submit the new bid.}
    \ENDIF

\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}
\section{Additional Liar's dice analyses}\label{A: unfair dice}
\subsection{The ratio of successful challenges}\label{A: additional analyses}
As mentioned in Section \ref{sec: bluffing}, a more deceitful playing style of the neural ODE-based players does not seem to increase the ratio of successful challenges. To support this claim, we reproduce Figure \ref{fig: analyses}b and compare it to the ratio of successful challenges presented in Figure \ref{fig:ib_vs_cr}b. When considering the ratio of successful challenges for $\lambda_0 \leq 6$, the neural ODE-based agents (orange) have a smaller ratio than the purely MFG-based agents (blue). The only exception is found at $\lambda_0 = 0$, where the ratio of successful challenges for the MFG-based agents is very low due to the total absence of bluffing (Figure \ref{fig:ib_vs_cr}a). However, when $\lambda_0 > 6$, the success ratio of the neural ODE agents is larger than that of the MFG-based agents, even though the strategies of both groups are almost equal (Figure \ref{fig:ib_vs_cr}a). 
