\begin{abstract}

% v1

% The extended Berkeley Packet Filter (eBPF) has gained significant traction in various domains, including cloud-native computing, performance monitoring, and security applications. Its ability to execute sandboxed programs within the Linux kernel has revolutionized the way we observe, control, and enhance system behavior. However, despite the growing ecosystem of eBPF applications, there remains a critical gap in the management, distribution, loading, and execution of arbitrary eBPF source code on arbitrary kernel and its associated user-space control and data processing programs. WebAssembly provides fine-grained sandbox ability for protecting both the control flow and memory safety. This paper presents a comprehensive framework, Wasm-BPF, by combining the Wasm and BPF that addresses the challenges of managing, distributing, loading, and executing eBPF programs and their corresponding user-space components. The proposed framework consists of several key components. First, it includes a centralized repository for storing and versioning eBPF source code and related artifacts, facilitating easy distribution and collaboration among developers. Second, it incorporates a secure and efficient mechanism for compiling and validating eBPF programs, ensuring their safety and compatibility with the target environment. Third, the framework provides a unified WASI API for loading and attaching eBPF programs to various kernel hooks, enabling seamless integration with existing systems. Fourth, it offers a scalable and resilient runtime environment for executing user-space isolation control and data processing applications, ensuring high availability and performance. Our results show that by extending the Wasm Runtime with BPF tracing and security capability, we have only 2\% overhead compared to natively running the same bcc program.

% Current deployment strategies for eBPF programs present various challenges. Deployments as standalone containers in K8s are overly cumbersome, and others require a controlling daemon. Additionally, deploying eBPF applications across different kernel versions, operating systems and archetect raises difficultly. Furthermore, comprehensive observability agents (like Deepflow and Pixie) need capabilities for dynamic plugin management based on varying scenarios, which traditional tools do not adequately support.

