\section{Numerical Illustrations}
\label{sec:num}

In this section, we compare the performance of the proposed methods against the original multiderivative schemes on a classical Hamiltonian test problem, with a particular focus on conservation properties. In addition to the newly derived schemes, we also consider four related methods from \cite{Mathematica2021}, which are based on the MDTR4 and MDMP4 methods. These methods differ in how they approximate  the extra stages required by the discretization of the Lie derivatives  \eqref{diffMPa} and \eqref{diffMPb}: 
\begin{itemize}
    \item AMDMP4\_TR2 and AMDTR4\_TR2 use the trapezoidal scheme; they form a symplectic/conjugate-symplectic twin;
    \item AMDMP4\_R2 and AMDTR4\_R2 are based on the classical second-order Runge--Kutta scheme. they does not yield a symplectic/conjugate-symplectic couple.
\end{itemize}

We consider the planar Kepler problem, governed by the system
$$
\dot{q} = p, \qquad \dot{p} = -\|q\|_2^{-3} q, \qquad q = (q_1, q_2)^\top, \quad p = (p_1, p_2)^\top \in \mathbb{R}^2,
$$
with initial conditions
\begin{equation}
\label{kep}
q(0) = 
\left(1-e,  0 \right)^\top, 
\qquad p(0) =  \left( 0,  \sqrt{\frac{1+e}{1-e}}\right)^\top, \qquad e \in[0,1).
\end{equation}
This yields a periodic elliptical orbit of period $T = 2\pi$, with eccentricity $e$. For a complete discussion of the problem, see \cite{HLW06, BI16}. In our tests, we adopt the same parameters as in \cite{Mathematica2021}, namely:
\begin{itemize}
    \item eccentricity: $e = 0.6$
    \item integration interval: $[0,  10^{3}\cdot T]$
    \item time step: $h = T / 200$.
\end{itemize}
All simulations were performed in MATLAB R2024b. The nonlinear systems arising from the implicit methods were solved using a modified Newton method with a fixed Jacobian. 

In the first experiment,  we show how small perturbations in the parameter $\alpha$ used in the computation of Lie derivatives affect the conservation properties. Specifically, we consider perturbed values of $\alpha$ around the {\em symplectic value} $\alpha = \sqrt{3}/6$, by scaling it via factors $\gamma$ and $1/\gamma$, and evaluate the error in angular momentum $M(y)$: the reference value is $M(y_0)$ for  AMDMP4\_C2 and $M(y_{1/2})$ for  AMDTR4\_C2. The meaning of this latter choice is that, at the midpoints $(t_n +t_{n+1})/2$ the AMDTR4\_C2 method is tantamount to AMDMP4\_C2 integrator.

