% In this paper, our analysis reveals that the POS tag of a preceding token critically influences the probability of an End-of-Sequence (EOS) token appearing next. Based on this insight, we propose the \textbf{POS-Aware Delay Mechanism}. This mechanism concentrates attention on tokens more likely to lead to an EOS, refining EOS token suppression by utilizing weights derived from preceding token POS tags. It actively counteracts the model's natural tendency to terminate when linguistic cues suggest a conclusion. Furthermore, to maximize resource consumption, we argue that inducing repetitive loops by constraining output diversity is more effective for sustained generation. To achieve this, we introduce a \textbf{Generative Path Pruning Mechanism}. This component directly constrains the magnitude of internal hidden state representations, encouraging them towards a concentrated region to actively promote sustained, high-volume looping patterns. We synergistically combine these two components into our novel \textbf{LingoLoop Attack}. On Qwen2.5-VL-3B model, our method produces \textbf{30$\times$} more output tokens and consumes \textbf{30$\times$} more energy compared to clean inputs.
% Extensive experiments further show that LingoLoop consistently drives MLLMs to produce outputs approaching their maximum generation limits.
Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) have shown great promise but require substantial computational resources during inference. Attackers can exploit this by inducing excessive output, leading to resource exhaustion and service degradation. Prior energy-latency attacks aim to increase generation time by broadly shifting the output token distribution away from the EOS token, but they neglect the influence of token-level Part-of-Speech (POS) characteristics on EOS and sentence-level structural patterns on output counts, limiting their efficacy. To address this, we propose \textbf{LingoLoop}, an attack designed to induce MLLMs to generate excessively verbose and repetitive sequences. First, we find that the POS tag of a token strongly affects the likelihood of generating an EOS token. Based on this insight, we propose a \textbf{POS-Aware Delay Mechanism} to postpone EOS token generation by adjusting attention weights guided by POS information. Second, we identify that constraining output diversity to induce repetitive loops is effective for sustained generation. We introduce a \textbf{Generative Path Pruning Mechanism} that limits the magnitude of hidden states, encouraging the model to produce persistent loops. Extensive experiments demonstrate LingoLoop can increase generated tokens by up to \textbf{30$\times$} and energy consumption by a comparable factor on models like Qwen2.5-VL-3B, consistently driving MLLMs towards their maximum generation limits. These findings expose significant MLLMs' vulnerabilities, posing challenges for their reliable deployment. The code will be released publicly following the paper’s acceptance.
\end{abstract}


